WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.988 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.988 --> 00:00:03.479 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.479 --> 00:00:04.970 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:16.655 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.655 --> 00:00:18.530 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: OK,
anyway, so online markets.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:23.000 align:middle line:84%
So what I've got
several different papers

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.820 align:middle line:90%
I figured I'd talk about today.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.030 align:middle line:84%
All of them are talking
about online retail

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:33.670 align:middle line:84%
in one form or another
and just different issues

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.110 align:middle line:84%
in online retail trying to
understand the industry.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.910 align:middle line:84%
So the first paper I was going
to talk about, Einav, Kuchler,

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:41.800 align:middle line:84%
Levin, Sundaresan,
Learning from Seller

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.600 align:middle line:90%
Experiments in Online Markets.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.980 align:middle line:90%
This is a paper studying eBay.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:52.570 align:middle line:84%
And while eBay has been
in a long run decline,

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.430 align:middle line:84%
it's still an important way
in which things get sold.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:56.860 align:middle line:84%
And I also just
wanted to cover it

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:01.120 align:middle line:84%
because I felt the
methodology of the paper

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.690 align:middle line:84%
is interesting as a way to
attack problems and these ideas

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:08.470 align:middle line:84%
that what they're talking about
is an alternative to doing field

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:12.340 align:middle line:84%
experiments or setting up
agreements to run experiments

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.290 align:middle line:84%
with online service
providers is what they

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:17.870 align:middle line:90%
call opportunistic experiments.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.040 align:middle line:84%
There are many, many
businesses in this world.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.830 align:middle line:84%
Those businesses are trying
to operate optimally.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.170 align:middle line:84%
They run lots of
experiments themselves.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.310 align:middle line:84%
And so their idea
is to take advantage

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.010 align:middle line:84%
of all these experiments that
sellers are running on eBay

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:32.430 align:middle line:90%
and analyze those.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.593 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, you know, you
get less controlled experiments

00:01:35.593 --> 00:01:37.760 align:middle line:84%
than if you were setting
up the experiment yourself.

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.020 align:middle line:84%
Each experiment is not ideal,
but, you know, there's thousands

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.910 align:middle line:84%
and thousands of not
ideal experiments

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:48.320 align:middle line:84%
may be more
informative and cheaper

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.140 align:middle line:84%
to run than just one
experiment you design yourself.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.970 align:middle line:84%
So to start with, they
note that, you know,

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.690 align:middle line:84%
very few things on eBay
are actually unique.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:59.720 align:middle line:84%
They said, you know, I guess
interesting fact about eBay,

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.450 align:middle line:84%
for about a half of the
items that are sold on eBay,

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:04.730 align:middle line:84%
you can find a nearly
identical item sold

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:07.259 align:middle line:90%
by exactly the same seller.

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:09.259 align:middle line:84%
And so when they're talking
about an experiment,

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:14.270 align:middle line:84%
an experiment meaning you
have exactly the same seller

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:17.210 align:middle line:84%
selling a product with
exactly the same listing

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.190 align:middle line:84%
title listed in the same
category and subtitle.

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:23.540 align:middle line:84%
And they find 55 million
seller experiments

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.680 align:middle line:84%
involving a total of
350 million listings.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.473 align:middle line:84%
For much of their
analysis, they're

00:02:30.473 --> 00:02:32.390 align:middle line:84%
restricting attention
to experiments that have

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.020 align:middle line:90%
several different features.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.970 align:middle line:84%
There are at least
two items auctioned.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.885 align:middle line:84%
There's also at least one
successful posted price sale.

00:02:38.885 --> 00:02:40.760 align:middle line:84%
They want a posted price
sale because they're

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.590 align:middle line:84%
going to use that as
a reference for what's

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:44.550 align:middle line:84%
the market price of this
if you just sell it.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:48.000 align:middle line:90%
So not through an auction.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.580 align:middle line:84%
And even with
those restrictions,

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:53.910 align:middle line:84%
they come up with 244,000
experiments that have 7.6

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.440 align:middle line:90%
million listings.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.370 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is just that
eBay sellers are just

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.110 align:middle line:84%
experimenting all the time
with all kinds of things.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.200 align:middle line:84%
And this is something we
can take advantage of.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:09.350 align:middle line:84%
The paper itself has multiple
different sub papers discussing

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:10.320 align:middle line:90%
what to do with this.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.570 align:middle line:84%
So one of the sub papers
is how do "Buy It Now"

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:16.460 align:middle line:90%
prices affect outcomes?

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.790 align:middle line:84%
On eBay, if you've never
been on an eBay auction,

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:22.030 align:middle line:84%
it's like it's classic
English ascending bid auction,

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.190 align:middle line:84%
but there's also this
option of just click

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:27.350 align:middle line:84%
here to buy it for $125
and end the auction.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:28.900 align:middle line:84%
And so one of the
papers they do is

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.310 align:middle line:84%
how does having a Buy It Now
price affect the outcome?

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:36.230 align:middle line:84%
Second is, how does shipping
fees affect outcomes?

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:40.540 align:middle line:84%
There's this interesting earlier
paper by Hossain and Morgan

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:44.560 align:middle line:84%
that argued that consumers
are fairly naive in dealing

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.590 align:middle line:84%
with shipping fees and that
you make more money by having

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:52.430 align:middle line:84%
good with $9.95 shipping than
good with $5.95 shipping.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:56.495 align:middle line:84%
Just somehow you offer the same
good, different shipping prices,

00:03:56.495 --> 00:03:58.120 align:middle line:84%
people somehow focus
on the item price,

00:03:58.120 --> 00:03:59.210 align:middle line:90%
not on the shipping price.

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:00.918 align:middle line:84%
And so the plus shipping
and handling you

00:04:00.918 --> 00:04:04.480 align:middle line:84%
make more money selling with a
high shipping and handling fee.

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.390 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, they're going to
do an experiment on that

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:09.545 align:middle line:84%
or many sellers have
done experiments on that.

00:04:09.545 --> 00:04:10.920 align:middle line:84%
And then they also
have this sort

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.410 align:middle line:84%
of third paper about how and
why the reserve prices affect

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:15.500 align:middle line:90%
revenues.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.600 align:middle line:84%
I'm just going to talk
about the third one

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.269 align:middle line:84%
because it feeds back to things
I talked about in the auction

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:24.780 align:middle line:84%
literature about optimal
reserve prices and auctions.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.330 align:middle line:84%
OK, here's just their
motivating figure

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.270 align:middle line:90%
to explain eBay experiments.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:34.710 align:middle line:84%
Like, so they did a search
for "TaylorMade driver."

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.155 align:middle line:84%
TaylorMade is a brand of
golf club manufacturer.

00:04:38.155 --> 00:04:39.780 align:middle line:84%
You do a search for
"TaylorMade driver"

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.410 align:middle line:84%
on eBay and you just see a
screen like this just full

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:49.360 align:middle line:84%
of people selling new
and used golf clubs.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.180 align:middle line:84%
And it looks like
there's just a zillion

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.260 align:middle line:84%
different golf clubs
out there on eBay.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.160 align:middle line:84%
But if you do a more
specific search.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.420 align:middle line:84%
So for this specific search,
they typed in "TaylorMade burner

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:05.700 align:middle line:84%
09 driver 2009 golf
club new quote 10RH."

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.890 align:middle line:84%
And so you do this
very specific search

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.240 align:middle line:90%
and then what do you find?

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:11.520 align:middle line:84%
You find that there
is one seller who

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.180 align:middle line:84%
uses that as his title when he
sells TaylorMade drivers online.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.000 align:middle line:84%
And so all the searches say
"TaylorMade burner 09 driver

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.990 align:middle line:84%
2009 golf club new 10.5
degrees right hand."

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:24.460 align:middle line:84%
And what you'll
notice in this thing

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:27.080 align:middle line:84%
is that the seller has
these many, many auctions.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.600 align:middle line:84%
This one is closing in
3 hours and 21 minutes.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.850 align:middle line:84%
This one closes in 3
hours and 22 minutes.

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:34.070 align:middle line:84%
This one closes in 6
hours and 9 minutes.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:35.325 align:middle line:90%
6 hours and 9 minutes.

00:05:35.325 --> 00:05:35.950 align:middle line:90%
8 hours and 59.

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:38.380 align:middle line:90%
9, 41.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:41.020 align:middle line:84%
So this seller is selling
auctioning off eight

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.010 align:middle line:90%
of these in the next 12 hours.

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:46.030 align:middle line:84%
And you can see this seller
is conducting an experiment.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:49.070 align:middle line:84%
This golf club here-- maybe
you can't see it, I can.

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:51.020 align:middle line:90%
This one has $7.99 shipping.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:53.410 align:middle line:90%
This one has $9.99 shipping.

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:55.190 align:middle line:90%
This one has $9.99 shipping.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:56.750 align:middle line:90%
This one has $7.99 shipping.

00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:00.230 align:middle line:84%
So here it's clear these
have Buy It Now prices.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.740 align:middle line:90%
This is just $125 plus $9.99.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:05.240 align:middle line:90%
This $125 plus $7.99.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.180 align:middle line:84%
If anyone saw
these two listings,

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.380 align:middle line:84%
obviously no one
would buy this one.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:12.290 align:middle line:84%
But they're just mixed
in with that big list.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:14.240 align:middle line:84%
People may only see
one versus the other.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.160 align:middle line:84%
It's unclear what's
going to happen.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.170 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so this is one
seller's experiment.

00:06:21.170 --> 00:06:23.650 align:middle line:84%
And their idea is that
there are many, many,

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:25.160 align:middle line:84%
many of these
experiments out there.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:32.475 align:middle line:84%
But this is one of the
244,000 they're analyzing.

00:06:32.475 --> 00:06:34.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What's the logic
from the seller's perspective?

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:36.880 align:middle line:90%
Like, where does it come from?

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.510 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: [INAUDIBLE] well,
so I guess the seller probably

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:44.200 align:middle line:84%
doesn't know whether a $7.99
shipping or a $9.99 shipping

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.420 align:middle line:90%
produces better profits.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:48.698 align:middle line:84%
So they're probably just
auctioning both of them off,

00:06:48.698 --> 00:06:50.740 align:middle line:84%
and they may have been
doing this for a few weeks

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:54.850 align:middle line:84%
and then stopped and settled
on whichever one was better.

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:57.860 align:middle line:84%
Certainly this Buy It
Now versus the auction,

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.770 align:middle line:84%
they're probably some people
who prefer one to the other.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:02.520 align:middle line:84%
And so you probably
sell both ways.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:07.070 align:middle line:90%


00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.290 align:middle line:84%
But, you know, something
you see in their data

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.365 align:middle line:84%
is you see people
conducting auctions

00:07:12.365 --> 00:07:14.990 align:middle line:84%
where the starting price in the
auction is above the Buy It Now

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:16.400 align:middle line:90%
price.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.400 align:middle line:84%
So it does seem like there are
people who just see an auction

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.920 align:middle line:84%
and decide to enter it and other
people who just are looking

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.470 align:middle line:84%
for a fixed price and buy it
and not really paying attention

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:25.450 align:middle line:90%
to the opposite ones.

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:33.570 align:middle line:90%


00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:37.560 align:middle line:84%
OK, anyway, so how do we think
about effects of reserve prices

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.630 align:middle line:90%
in IPV auctions?

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:44.568 align:middle line:84%
If you remember basic
theory of reserve

00:07:44.568 --> 00:07:46.360 align:middle line:84%
prices in an English
auction is the reserve

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:49.430 align:middle line:84%
price in English auction is the
monopoly price to one bidder.

00:07:49.430 --> 00:07:52.410 align:middle line:84%
And the logic was just,
you're selling this good,

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.160 align:middle line:84%
once you found out
what the second highest

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.240 align:middle line:84%
value is, the willingness to
pay of the high value bidder

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.070 align:middle line:84%
is just the
distribution truncated

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:02.630 align:middle line:84%
at the point at which you know
their value is at least that.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:05.620 align:middle line:84%
And so you're just monopoly
pricing on the single bidder

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:07.210 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.872 align:middle line:84%
And so it's just the
optimal reserve price

00:08:09.872 --> 00:08:11.080 align:middle line:90%
is the monopoly price, right?

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:16.460 align:middle line:84%
You have this
distribution of values.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:19.060 align:middle line:84%
Once everyone else
has dropped out here,

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:21.460 align:middle line:84%
all you know is
that you're selling

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:27.420 align:middle line:84%
to someone whose draw is from
this is the second highest bid.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:30.380 align:middle line:90%


00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:31.809 align:middle line:90%
Nope.

00:08:31.809 --> 00:08:32.309 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:34.190 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so you have
this distribution

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:35.539 align:middle line:90%
of the second highest bid.

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:36.559 align:middle line:90%
You're pricing to that.

00:08:36.559 --> 00:08:39.860 align:middle line:84%
You set the monopoly price
on that demand curve.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:42.710 align:middle line:84%
They also note that if we want
to think about this empirically,

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:46.010 align:middle line:84%
how does a reserve
price effect--

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:47.670 align:middle line:84%
what's the optimal
reserve price?

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:49.760 align:middle line:84%
Here's another good
way to think about--

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:53.660 align:middle line:84%
so this is density F of v.
If you want to think about

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:56.180 align:middle line:84%
how does a reserve price
affect things, what I can do

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.890 align:middle line:84%
is just imagine an
experiment where

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:01.040 align:middle line:84%
the seller has used many,
many different reserve prices.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:03.140 align:middle line:84%
Like, they've
started the bidding

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:06.680 align:middle line:90%
at $1, $5, $10, 20, 30, 40, 50.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.800 align:middle line:84%
For every reserve
price, you can just

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:14.090 align:middle line:84%
draw a graph, which
is, if I use a reserve

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:20.600 align:middle line:84%
price of 5% of the
expected sale price,

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:25.010 align:middle line:84%
what's the eventual price
at which I sell the good,

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:29.310 align:middle line:90%
and what's the--

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:31.500 align:middle line:84%
if I use a very
low reserve price,

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:35.760 align:middle line:84%
I sell the good with
probability 0.9 or 0.95,

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:38.440 align:middle line:84%
but I get a fairly
low average price.

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:41.550 align:middle line:84%
And then if I sell it
with a $20 reserve,

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:44.550 align:middle line:84%
maybe I sell the good
with probability 0.8

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.600 align:middle line:84%
and I get a higher
average price.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:48.720 align:middle line:84%
And if I sell it
with a reserve that's

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:51.820 align:middle line:84%
120% of the average
price for the good,

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:54.450 align:middle line:84%
maybe I sell it with just
some very small probability,

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.623 align:middle line:84%
but I do get a very
high price of selling.

00:09:56.623 --> 00:09:59.040 align:middle line:84%
So what you can basically do
is just look at every reserve

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.560 align:middle line:84%
price in the data
and figure out what's

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.540 align:middle line:84%
the probability
that the good sells

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:06.820 align:middle line:84%
and what's the expected price
conditional on its selling,

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:09.972 align:middle line:84%
and that's basically
just your demand curve.

00:10:09.972 --> 00:10:11.430 align:middle line:84%
You can view that
as a demand curve

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.930 align:middle line:84%
and you're just trying to
monopoly price on that curve.

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:18.660 align:middle line:84%
So that's a second way to think
about optimal reserve pricing is

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:21.360 align:middle line:84%
just draw this demand
curve like object

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:24.417 align:middle line:84%
and think about what would be
the monopoly price on that.

00:10:24.417 --> 00:10:26.250 align:middle line:84%
And that would be the
way to figure out what

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.420 align:middle line:90%
the optimal reserve price is.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:30.000 align:middle line:84%
So that would be
an experimental way

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.910 align:middle line:84%
to figure out my optimal reserve
price without, you know--

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:34.420 align:middle line:84%
theoretically you
want to just say,

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:35.970 align:middle line:84%
what's the distribution
from which the values are

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:36.630 align:middle line:90%
being drawn.

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:38.490 align:middle line:84%
But without thinking about what
the distribution from which

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:41.130 align:middle line:84%
values are being drawn, you
could just make this curve

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:44.880 align:middle line:90%
monopoly price relative to it.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.161 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:46.161 --> 00:10:49.050 align:middle line:84%
You know, several reasons why
this simple theory may not

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:50.760 align:middle line:90%
work in practice.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:52.540 align:middle line:90%
One is entry costs.

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:54.540 align:middle line:84%
If you imagine there's
some cost to a bidder

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:58.740 align:middle line:84%
of starting to bid in
an auction and examining

00:10:58.740 --> 00:10:59.940 align:middle line:90%
the good in detail.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:01.710 align:middle line:84%
If there's a low
price, they may be

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:03.190 align:middle line:90%
willing to pay the entry cost.

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:05.340 align:middle line:84%
If they see that the
reserve price is $110,

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.320 align:middle line:84%
they may not bother to
read all the fine print

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:10.320 align:middle line:90%
and enter that auction.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:12.700 align:middle line:84%
There could be some kind
of common value component.

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:16.200 align:middle line:84%
This theory was, once I
know everybody else's bid,

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:17.760 align:middle line:90%
my bid is unchanged.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:19.720 align:middle line:84%
There's common values,
the theory changes.

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:22.470 align:middle line:84%
And then they also note
that there could be--

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:26.130 align:middle line:84%
speaking of this theory and
why this theory may not work.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:29.590 align:middle line:84%
You could also have behavioral
preferences, like loss aversion.

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:33.270 align:middle line:84%
If you imagine the loss
aversion is always like,

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:35.208 align:middle line:84%
I feel bad losing
something that I have.

00:11:35.208 --> 00:11:37.500 align:middle line:84%
If you start the bidding at
a very low price and people

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:39.480 align:middle line:84%
get used to the idea of
owning this fancy golf

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:41.585 align:middle line:84%
club because they see
it being bid for $20,

00:11:41.585 --> 00:11:42.960 align:middle line:84%
once they've got
used to the fact

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:45.670 align:middle line:84%
that they have this
fancy golf club,

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:49.293 align:middle line:84%
then they may feel loss aversion
if they don't win the golf club.

00:11:49.293 --> 00:11:51.460 align:middle line:84%
You might, by getting people
tied up in the bidding,

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:53.252 align:middle line:84%
they may get used to
the thought of winning

00:11:53.252 --> 00:11:56.440 align:middle line:84%
and then they may
bid more in the end.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:01.960 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, to examine this, what
they do is they find a data set

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:04.450 align:middle line:84%
where they found
19,000 experiments,

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:07.390 align:middle line:84%
where there are
several features.

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:10.930 align:middle line:84%
One is there's variation
in the start price,

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:13.580 align:middle line:84%
there's free shipping, which
just simplifies things.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:16.340 align:middle line:84%
There's no secret reserve
price, no Buy It Now auction.

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:20.770 align:middle line:84%
So these are just straight,
plain vanilla auctions

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:23.050 align:middle line:90%
of a good, many different times.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:30.570 align:middle line:84%
And so about 25
listings per experiment.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:35.280 align:middle line:84%
So again, these
are just, you know,

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:39.910 align:middle line:84%
sellers experimenting with,
if I'm selling $125 golf club,

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:43.135 align:middle line:84%
should I start the bidding
at $5, or $100, or 50,

00:12:43.135 --> 00:12:44.135 align:middle line:90%
or something in between?

00:12:44.135 --> 00:12:46.640 align:middle line:90%


00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:48.500 align:middle line:84%
Figure 5 of the paper
shows that, yes,

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:50.640 align:middle line:90%
sellers are doing this a lot.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:53.000 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at a typical
category like goods

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.610 align:middle line:90%
that cost between $30 and $100.

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:58.572 align:middle line:84%
And you can see goods
where that cost,

00:12:58.572 --> 00:13:00.530 align:middle line:84%
this is like the average
price at which they're

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:04.160 align:middle line:84%
sold when they sell them
through just a fixed price sale.

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:07.190 align:middle line:84%
And you can see goods
that sell for $30 to $100,

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:11.990 align:middle line:84%
20% of the sellers start them
out at 0 in these experiments

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:17.390 align:middle line:84%
and then 37% start them
out at between 85 to 100%

00:13:17.390 --> 00:13:19.520 align:middle line:90%
of the average fixed price sale.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:21.870 align:middle line:90%
17% choose 60 to 80%.

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:25.490 align:middle line:84%
13% are actually starting
them off above the average

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:28.080 align:middle line:84%
Buy It Now average
fixed price sales.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.780 align:middle line:84%
But we get this sort of
bimodal distribution.

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:33.513 align:middle line:84%
Notably, there are very few
sellers starting out in here.

00:13:33.513 --> 00:13:35.180 align:middle line:84%
It seems like if
you're going to go low,

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.100 align:middle line:84%
you just go all the
way down to the bottom

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:40.620 align:middle line:90%
and start at almost 0.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:44.380 align:middle line:84%
And then there's with-- a lot
of within experiment variation.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:48.270 align:middle line:84%
So if you look at auctions
in which their sellers are

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:50.950 align:middle line:84%
sometimes selling
it at less than 5%,

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:52.440 align:middle line:84%
those same sellers
were sometimes

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:56.280 align:middle line:84%
selling it at less than 5% are
also sometimes selling 5 to 45%,

00:13:56.280 --> 00:14:01.800 align:middle line:84%
45 to 85%, 85 to 100%, 100
to 120%, greater than 120%.

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:03.630 align:middle line:84%
So there are just
many experiments

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:07.450 align:middle line:84%
where sellers are trying out
the whole range themselves.

00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:10.412 align:middle line:84%
This is our experimental
paper that we

00:14:10.412 --> 00:14:12.120 align:middle line:84%
get to run without
acting to actually pay

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:13.026 align:middle line:90%
for the experiments.

00:14:13.026 --> 00:14:17.040 align:middle line:90%


00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:17.540 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:23.980 align:middle line:84%
So does the standard
auction demand theory work?

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:26.870 align:middle line:90%
And the answer is no.

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:28.880 align:middle line:84%
There's one interesting
feature, which

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:32.660 align:middle line:84%
is the demand curve turns
up at very low prices.

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.060 align:middle line:84%
So remember, I drew
this curve for you,

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:38.975 align:middle line:84%
saying, if I start
the good at only $5,

00:14:38.975 --> 00:14:40.100 align:middle line:90%
if I start the good at $1--

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:43.280 align:middle line:90%


00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:45.140 align:middle line:84%
if I start the good
at $1, I sell it

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:46.585 align:middle line:84%
almost entirely
for sure, but I'm

00:14:46.585 --> 00:14:48.710 align:middle line:84%
going to have a lower
average price than if I start

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:50.600 align:middle line:90%
the good at a higher price.

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:54.350 align:middle line:84%
What they find is that the
curve doesn't look like this.

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:56.200 align:middle line:90%
The curve looks like this.

00:14:56.200 --> 00:15:01.350 align:middle line:90%


00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:05.130 align:middle line:84%
So as I start at a lower
and lower price, not

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:08.400 align:middle line:84%
only do I sell with
a higher probability,

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:11.970 align:middle line:84%
I sell at a higher price,
conditional on selling.

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:15.018 align:middle line:84%
So I'm up here, I'm
selling at 90% probability

00:15:15.018 --> 00:15:16.560 align:middle line:84%
and I'm getting a
higher price than I

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:18.268 align:middle line:84%
would get if I chose
a reserve price that

00:15:18.268 --> 00:15:20.670 align:middle line:90%
only sold with 70% probability.

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:23.700 align:middle line:84%
So the demand curve
turns up, suggesting

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:28.510 align:middle line:84%
that I put the $100 item out
there, start the bidding at $1,

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:30.360 align:middle line:90%
lots of people see it at $1.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:32.040 align:middle line:84%
10 hours before the
auction is over,

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:33.970 align:middle line:84%
they all start
following the auction.

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:35.640 align:middle line:90%
They all start bidding.

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:40.840 align:middle line:84%
I get more entry than I would if
I started at it at $20 instead.

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:43.650 align:middle line:84%
So the curve, instead
of looking like this,

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:44.930 align:middle line:90%
looks like this instead.

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:50.940 align:middle line:90%


00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:52.860 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, how do we
do monopoly pricing?

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:54.930 align:middle line:84%
Like, the way to think
about monopoly pricing

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:57.220 align:middle line:84%
is you do marginal revenue
equals marginal cost.

00:15:57.220 --> 00:15:59.580 align:middle line:84%
So they take this
demand curve, they

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:01.830 align:middle line:84%
construct the marginal
revenue curve,

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:07.260 align:middle line:84%
and the marginal revenue
curve also turns up.

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:10.530 align:middle line:84%
Like, we're used to marginal
revenue is downward sloping.

00:16:10.530 --> 00:16:12.480 align:middle line:90%
You draw marginal cost across.

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:14.530 align:middle line:84%
Where marginal revenue
hits marginal cost,

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:17.620 align:middle line:90%
that's the monopoly price.

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:21.090 align:middle line:84%
What do you do when
marginal revenue turns up?

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:26.760 align:middle line:84%
So if this is my
marginal revenue curve,

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:28.290 align:middle line:90%
my marginal cost is flat.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:30.870 align:middle line:84%
That's my normal
monopoly pricing.

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:34.650 align:middle line:84%
What do I do if marginal
revenue turns up?

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:39.060 align:middle line:84%
Then I can draw the line across
where marginal revenue equals

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:40.620 align:middle line:90%
marginal cost.

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:44.800 align:middle line:84%
This is a locally
optimal monopoly price.

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:49.640 align:middle line:84%
But the solution is either going
to be this point or this point.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:51.600 align:middle line:84%
You know, it doesn't
have to intersect again.

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:56.010 align:middle line:84%
It could be that you get a,
here your revenue's increasing,

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.640 align:middle line:84%
so it may be that you
want to price there.

00:16:58.640 --> 00:17:00.560 align:middle line:90%
You may want to price here.

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:02.300 align:middle line:84%
That may be the
global optimum or this

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:03.410 align:middle line:84%
could be the global
optimum, but you're

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:04.618 align:middle line:90%
going to do one or the other.

00:17:04.618 --> 00:17:08.089 align:middle line:90%


00:17:08.089 --> 00:17:08.630 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:10.669 align:middle line:84%
And so that's, I guess,
in some sense the--

00:17:10.669 --> 00:17:13.900 align:middle line:90%


00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:17.150 align:middle line:84%
this is how they explain
what's going on in the data.

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:19.930 align:middle line:84%
If you remember in the data,
there was a lot of firms--

00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:20.920 align:middle line:90%
figure 5.

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:25.630 align:middle line:84%
Like, there were a lot of firms
charging fairly high reserve

00:17:25.630 --> 00:17:28.030 align:middle line:84%
prices and then some firms
charging very low reserve

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:29.090 align:middle line:90%
prices.

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:31.690 align:middle line:84%
And so their thought is that
what's going on in the data

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:34.840 align:middle line:84%
is probably something like
this, where the marginal cost is

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:35.960 align:middle line:90%
pretty high.

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:40.210 align:middle line:84%
So you either set a quite
high reserve price and this

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:43.100 align:middle line:84%
is the optimum or you
set this right end point.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:45.360 align:middle line:84%
And for different sellers,
different ones are optimal.

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:50.480 align:middle line:90%


00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.400 align:middle line:84%
And in general, if your
marginal cost is high,

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:54.400 align:middle line:84%
you're going to just set
the high reserve price.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:56.093 align:middle line:84%
Your marginal cost
is low, you're

00:17:56.093 --> 00:17:57.760 align:middle line:84%
going to be setting
the right end point.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.152 align:middle line:90%


00:18:00.152 --> 00:18:02.610 align:middle line:84%
You know, and then the paper
tries to do other predictions.

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:07.430 align:middle line:84%
It tries to have a test of
upper tail hypothesis of,

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:11.150 align:middle line:84%
does the upper tail of
the price distribution

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:14.850 align:middle line:84%
look the same regardless of
what reserve price you use?

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:17.220 align:middle line:84%
And that test only
works so well.

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:21.020 align:middle line:84%
So it does seem like
the classic theory,

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:24.140 align:middle line:84%
this sort of upper
tail distribution

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:25.610 align:middle line:84%
is just what you
get by truncating

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:27.470 align:middle line:90%
isn't quite right here.

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:29.480 align:middle line:90%
And it does seem to enter--

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:32.480 align:middle line:84%
it's entry costs are
important for online auctions,

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:36.730 align:middle line:84%
I think is the
main message there.

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:38.090 align:middle line:90%
OK, questions?

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:43.010 align:middle line:90%


00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:43.510 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:48.150 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, I think
that's something, you know--

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:50.850 align:middle line:90%
that work was done inside eBay.

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:52.620 align:middle line:84%
For a long time,
eBay actually had

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:56.598 align:middle line:84%
this sort of very, very
open policy to academics.

00:18:56.598 --> 00:18:58.140 align:middle line:84%
And we actually had
several students,

00:18:58.140 --> 00:18:59.940 align:middle line:84%
like, after they
finished their PhDs here,

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:02.760 align:middle line:84%
go to a postdoc for a year and
stay at eBay because eBay would

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:06.120 align:middle line:84%
let you sit there and
operate inside the company

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:08.670 align:middle line:84%
and do all kinds of stuff
with their full data set.

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:12.820 align:middle line:84%
That seems to have faded
away three or four years ago.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:14.820 align:middle line:84%
But obviously one could
still scrape eBay and do

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:17.430 align:middle line:90%
the same kinds of things.

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:21.732 align:middle line:84%
OK, so then the second
topic that I guess

00:19:21.732 --> 00:19:23.190 align:middle line:84%
is common to the
rest of the papers

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:26.370 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to talk about
is just understanding

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:30.990 align:middle line:84%
how online retail works
and why it's successful.

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:34.980 align:middle line:84%
You know, if you read people
writing about E-commerce

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:39.060 align:middle line:84%
early in the internet era,
there was this question of,

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:42.750 align:middle line:84%
is online retail ever going to
really take off and displace

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:44.410 align:middle line:90%
traditional retail?

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:47.520 align:middle line:84%
And the biggest argument
against online retail taking off

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:51.030 align:middle line:84%
was that it's just
much more expensive.

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:54.660 align:middle line:84%
You know, like, if you look
at the long history of retail

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:59.460 align:middle line:84%
in America, you
know, in the 1800s,

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:02.010 align:middle line:84%
people shopped in
department stores.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:06.270 align:middle line:84%
And then there was this
golden era of catalog retail

00:20:06.270 --> 00:20:09.690 align:middle line:84%
where, like the Sears
catalog in some sense

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:12.780 align:middle line:84%
brought products to-- you
know, they were only formally

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:14.770 align:middle line:84%
available in cities
to all of America.

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:16.890 align:middle line:84%
And they had like this
phone book, you know,

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:20.260 align:middle line:84%
1,000-page thick catalog
that they would mail you.

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:22.830 align:middle line:84%
You could buy anything
from the Sears catalog.

00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:26.550 align:middle line:84%
You know, the Sears catalog
just took off in the, I guess

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:29.790 align:middle line:84%
it's late 1800s after there
were trains, or whatever,

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:32.770 align:middle line:84%
that would bring
packages to people.

00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:35.890 align:middle line:84%
When the car was invented,
the Sears catalog just

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:37.420 align:middle line:90%
went into a prolonged decline.

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:41.290 align:middle line:84%
And Sears opened, you know,
massive, you know, not the size

00:20:41.290 --> 00:20:44.350 align:middle line:84%
of a Walmart, but, you know,
massive department stores

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:45.650 align:middle line:90%
in many US cities.

00:20:45.650 --> 00:20:47.840 align:middle line:84%
And again, the catalog
business declined.

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:51.940 align:middle line:84%
The physical retail
business took off.

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:54.190 align:middle line:90%
Thoughts on why that was.

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:57.310 align:middle line:84%
Catalog retail was just
always much more expensive

00:20:57.310 --> 00:20:58.770 align:middle line:90%
than traditional retail.

00:20:58.770 --> 00:21:04.820 align:middle line:84%
Like, if you look at Walmart,
Walmart's cost of selling goods

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:11.030 align:middle line:84%
is roughly 20% of the
merchandise that they sell.

00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:15.650 align:middle line:84%
Catalog retail had costs
more like 40 or 50%.

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:19.190 align:middle line:84%
And why does it cost
Walmart only 20%

00:21:19.190 --> 00:21:22.260 align:middle line:84%
when it costs
catalog retail 50%?

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:25.900 align:middle line:84%
You know, it's because
catalog retail,

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:27.760 align:middle line:84%
you need to have
stuff in a warehouse.

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:29.580 align:middle line:84%
Every single item
that gets sold,

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:32.580 align:middle line:84%
someone needs to put it in a
box, put the box in the mail,

00:21:32.580 --> 00:21:33.890 align:middle line:90%
pay for the mail.

00:21:33.890 --> 00:21:37.440 align:middle line:84%
Whereas Walmart's
cost of putting--

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:39.220 align:middle line:84%
like, the simplest
example of things

00:21:39.220 --> 00:21:40.387 align:middle line:90%
that should never be online.

00:21:40.387 --> 00:21:41.880 align:middle line:84%
Like, a kiddie
swimming pool, one

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:44.520 align:middle line:84%
of those plastic four-foot
diameter swimming pools.

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:47.580 align:middle line:84%
You can put a whole bunch of
four-foot diameter plastic

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:50.780 align:middle line:84%
swimming pools, stack them
together, put them in a truck,

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:53.280 align:middle line:84%
take them to the Walmart, and
the cost of getting them there

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:55.780 align:middle line:84%
is, you know, $0.50 or
something like that.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:58.170 align:middle line:84%
You try to put a kiddie
swimming pool in the mail

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.120 align:middle line:84%
and send it to
somebody, you've spent,

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:03.210 align:middle line:84%
you know, $30 mailing
something that's only worth

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:04.660 align:middle line:90%
$5 or something.

00:22:04.660 --> 00:22:06.360 align:middle line:84%
And, you know, putting
things in a truck

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:10.320 align:middle line:84%
is just much, much cheaper
than handling every package

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:10.930 align:middle line:90%
individually.

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:13.020 align:middle line:84%
And you're getting some sense
the free labor of people

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:15.353 align:middle line:84%
to just walk through the store
and take it off the shelf

00:22:15.353 --> 00:22:18.130 align:middle line:90%
and carry it home.

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:23.267 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, early on, Amazon was
undercutting Barnes & Noble

00:22:23.267 --> 00:22:25.600 align:middle line:84%
and selling books for lower
than you would buy the books

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:27.060 align:middle line:90%
in a physical bookstore.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:29.500 align:middle line:84%
And this question
of, could Amazon ever

00:22:29.500 --> 00:22:32.960 align:middle line:84%
actually make a profit
selling things not at a loss?

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:36.430 align:middle line:84%
And if Amazon were pricing
things at cost or above cost,

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:39.880 align:middle line:84%
could it be competitive
with traditional retail?

00:22:39.880 --> 00:22:43.450 align:middle line:84%
One of the big arguments
that was made early on

00:22:43.450 --> 00:22:46.030 align:middle line:84%
was that, yes,
Amazon doesn't need

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:48.770 align:middle line:84%
to have a cost advantage
to sell things online.

00:22:48.770 --> 00:22:51.310 align:middle line:84%
It could be that you have a
cost disadvantage that people

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:54.100 align:middle line:84%
buy online because of
the product variety.

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:57.010 align:middle line:84%
The product variety
advantages are much bigger

00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:59.030 align:middle line:90%
than the cost disadvantage.

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:02.057 align:middle line:84%
So therefore people buy online
to get the book they want

00:23:02.057 --> 00:23:03.640 align:middle line:84%
or whatever product
they want and have

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:06.070 align:middle line:90%
a much bigger selection.

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:08.770 align:middle line:84%
And they note that
when Amazon launched,

00:23:08.770 --> 00:23:10.530 align:middle line:90%
Amazon had one million titles.

00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:13.270 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, it later expanded
to several million titles.

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:17.060 align:middle line:84%
And a very large physical
bookstore at the time would have

00:23:17.060 --> 00:23:18.470 align:middle line:90%
100,000 titles.

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:22.820 align:middle line:84%
You're typical bookstore might
have 50,000 titles in it.

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:26.490 align:middle line:84%
Perhaps there's some superstores
that would have 200,000.

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:30.350 align:middle line:84%
But Amazon had many,
many more books,

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:35.395 align:middle line:84%
even from the very beginning,
than physical bookstores did.

00:23:35.395 --> 00:23:37.520 align:middle line:84%
And so what Brynjolfsson,
Hu, and Smith tried to do

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:41.270 align:middle line:84%
is just say, could we estimate
how big the product variety

00:23:41.270 --> 00:23:45.480 align:middle line:84%
benefit is by just, in some
sense, looking at this demand

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:45.980 align:middle line:90%
curve?

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:53.630 align:middle line:84%
Like, finding out what fraction
of the sales of Amazon are

00:23:53.630 --> 00:23:58.178 align:middle line:84%
to books that are beyond
the 100,000th book?

00:23:58.178 --> 00:24:00.470 align:middle line:84%
So let's say there are 100,000
books that are available

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:01.980 align:middle line:90%
in your physical bookstore.

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:03.410 align:middle line:84%
There are millions
of other books

00:24:03.410 --> 00:24:05.500 align:middle line:84%
that are not available
in a physical bookstore.

00:24:05.500 --> 00:24:09.350 align:middle line:84%
How much utility do people get
from buying all those books from

00:24:09.350 --> 00:24:12.320 align:middle line:90%
100,000 out to 2 million?

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:18.270 align:middle line:84%
And the way they do that is to
just basically convert Amazon

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:21.870 align:middle line:84%
sales ranks to quantities,
figure out how many copies

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:25.260 align:middle line:84%
of every book are being sold,
and then just count up how many

00:24:25.260 --> 00:24:28.150 align:middle line:84%
books-- what fraction of Amazon
sales are books outside the top

00:24:28.150 --> 00:24:31.800 align:middle line:84%
100,000 and then multiply them
by just a consumer surplus

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:33.090 align:middle line:90%
estimate per title.

00:24:33.090 --> 00:24:36.270 align:middle line:84%
If you have a general sense
of the demand elasticity,

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:38.490 align:middle line:84%
you know the average price,
you know the consumer,

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:39.990 align:middle line:84%
you can just guess
what the consumer

00:24:39.990 --> 00:24:43.850 align:middle line:90%
surplus is and estimate that.

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:47.360 align:middle line:84%
And what they show in this
figure is that if you look

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:50.150 align:middle line:84%
at books that are
outside the top 100,000,

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:52.910 align:middle line:84%
there seems to be this
very, very thick or very,

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:55.030 align:middle line:90%
very slow decline in sales.

00:24:55.030 --> 00:24:59.630 align:middle line:84%
So book number 100,000 is
selling 1 and 1/2 copies a week.

00:24:59.630 --> 00:25:02.100 align:middle line:84%
Book number 200,000
is selling 0.8.

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:05.400 align:middle line:84%
Book number 400 is
selling 0.6 copies a week.

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:08.460 align:middle line:84%
Book number 6 is
selling like 0.4.

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:10.650 align:middle line:84%
But then when you get
to book 2.2 million,

00:25:10.650 --> 00:25:13.580 align:middle line:84%
book 2.2 million is not selling
that many fewer copies than book

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:15.000 align:middle line:90%
600,000.

00:25:15.000 --> 00:25:20.390 align:middle line:84%
And so because this tail doesn't
just go to 0 very quickly,

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:22.430 align:middle line:84%
there are lots of
sales on Amazon

00:25:22.430 --> 00:25:24.620 align:middle line:90%
that are these extra books.

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:26.990 align:middle line:84%
And the thought is that
all these extra books

00:25:26.990 --> 00:25:31.190 align:middle line:84%
are contributing a lot
to consumer surplus.

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:32.960 align:middle line:84%
What they estimate
in their paper

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:36.590 align:middle line:84%
is that the product variety back
in 2000, when Amazon was still

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:40.760 align:middle line:84%
young, was adding $700
million to a billion

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:44.480 align:middle line:84%
to consumer surplus,
and that was about--

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:46.820 align:middle line:84%
Amazon was lowering
prices, I think it was by--

00:25:46.820 --> 00:25:50.730 align:middle line:84%
Amazon was 5 to 10% cheaper than
physical bookstores at the time.

00:25:50.730 --> 00:25:53.480 align:middle line:84%
And what they find is
that look at the gain

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:56.400 align:middle line:84%
from the lower prices,
the 5 to 10% lower prices.

00:25:56.400 --> 00:26:00.290 align:middle line:84%
Look at the gains from
the consumer surplus.

00:26:00.290 --> 00:26:02.060 align:middle line:84%
And the gains from
the lower prices

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:03.790 align:middle line:84%
were only about
$100 million a year.

00:26:03.790 --> 00:26:06.140 align:middle line:84%
The gains from the consumer
surplus is like $1 billion

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:06.910 align:middle line:90%
a year.

00:26:06.910 --> 00:26:11.310 align:middle line:84%
So consumer surplus is 10 times
as important as the price cut.

00:26:11.310 --> 00:26:14.330 align:middle line:84%
And so if you believe that, if
consumer surplus is 10 times as

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:17.780 align:middle line:84%
important as a price cut, you
could take away the $100 million

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:19.640 align:middle line:84%
or even have a
negative $100 million

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:22.850 align:middle line:84%
or negative $200 million, and
they say that Amazon really

00:26:22.850 --> 00:26:26.767 align:middle line:84%
could raise prices and still be
a positive value for consumers

00:26:26.767 --> 00:26:28.225 align:middle line:84%
because the product
variety effect.

00:26:28.225 --> 00:26:30.760 align:middle line:90%


00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:32.980 align:middle line:90%
It's a very nice paper.

00:26:32.980 --> 00:26:35.250 align:middle line:84%
The calculation itself
is a little speculative

00:26:35.250 --> 00:26:37.667 align:middle line:90%
in that it is a--

00:26:37.667 --> 00:26:39.000 align:middle line:90%
we don't have a lot of evidence.

00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:40.380 align:middle line:84%
Like, if you think
about it, like,

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:42.060 align:middle line:84%
any of these books that
are sold very rarely,

00:26:42.060 --> 00:26:44.010 align:middle line:84%
it's very hard to get much
evidence of what the demand

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:46.010 align:middle line:84%
curves look like for them
because they're hardly

00:26:46.010 --> 00:26:47.160 align:middle line:90%
selling any copies.

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:51.600 align:middle line:84%
You are extrapolating
on some things there.

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.240 align:middle line:84%
So then the paper I
want to talk about more

00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:59.830 align:middle line:84%
is Quan and Williams' Product
Variety, Market Heterogeneity.

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:02.670 align:middle line:84%
And this is an argument
that the Brynjolfsson, Hu,

00:27:02.670 --> 00:27:08.040 align:middle line:84%
and Smith paper may really be
overestimating product variety

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:09.220 align:middle line:90%
advantages.

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:10.950 align:middle line:84%
And the reason they
would argue this

00:27:10.950 --> 00:27:13.440 align:middle line:84%
could be really overestimating
profit variety advantages

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:15.750 align:middle line:84%
is because different
physical bookstores

00:27:15.750 --> 00:27:17.410 align:middle line:90%
carry different books.

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:18.990 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance,
you know, people

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:21.930 align:middle line:84%
at the time needed
to get textbooks

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:24.400 align:middle line:84%
for all of their graduate
economics classes.

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:27.550 align:middle line:84%
Where would you get textbooks
for graduate economics classes?

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:30.700 align:middle line:84%
None of them are in the top
100,000 most popular books

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:32.060 align:middle line:90%
in the United States.

00:27:32.060 --> 00:27:34.750 align:middle line:84%
However, you go to the
MIT Coop in the week

00:27:34.750 --> 00:27:36.250 align:middle line:84%
before classes
start, and they would

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:38.380 align:middle line:84%
have all of the graduate
economics textbooks

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:41.140 align:middle line:84%
and all of the other textbooks
for all of your other classes

00:27:41.140 --> 00:27:42.680 align:middle line:90%
would be in the MIT Coop.

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:45.820 align:middle line:84%
And, you know, not just the
graduate economics textbooks

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:48.940 align:middle line:84%
from MIT Coop, but
the MIT Coop also

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:52.760 align:middle line:84%
has a big selection of,
you know, technical books.

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:55.600 align:middle line:84%
And if you just want books
about computer programming

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:57.550 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, not
for your classes,

00:27:57.550 --> 00:27:59.875 align:middle line:84%
they have lots of those
books in that bookstores.

00:27:59.875 --> 00:28:01.750 align:middle line:84%
And then there are other
bookstores in Boston

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:03.220 align:middle line:84%
where if you're
interested in art

00:28:03.220 --> 00:28:05.920 align:middle line:84%
that have lots of books
about art and lots

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:07.642 align:middle line:84%
of big coffee table
books or whatever.

00:28:07.642 --> 00:28:09.850 align:middle line:84%
And so their thought was
that-- the Quan and Williams

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:12.582 align:middle line:84%
argument is that you really
could be overestimating what

00:28:12.582 --> 00:28:15.040 align:middle line:84%
Amazon is doing because there
are many different bookstores

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:17.373 align:middle line:84%
and many different bookstores
have many different books.

00:28:17.373 --> 00:28:20.813 align:middle line:84%
So the gain from the online
over going to the bookstore

00:28:20.813 --> 00:28:22.230 align:middle line:84%
that you know would
have the books

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:24.840 align:middle line:90%
you want could be much smaller.

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:27.820 align:middle line:84%
They study this not
in the case of books.

00:28:27.820 --> 00:28:29.230 align:middle line:90%
They pick a different industry.

00:28:29.230 --> 00:28:31.350 align:middle line:90%
Their industry is shoes.

00:28:31.350 --> 00:28:34.000 align:middle line:84%
They don't say it, one
suspects that they have--

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:36.690 align:middle line:84%
they had a friend who worked at
Zappos, because they are working

00:28:36.690 --> 00:28:41.850 align:middle line:84%
with what they describe as
a very large online shoe

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:45.600 align:middle line:90%
store in the year 2012, 2013.

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:48.480 align:middle line:84%
And just here's
one example of why

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:52.350 align:middle line:84%
we would think that
physical shoe stores would

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:53.500 align:middle line:90%
carry different things.

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:59.790 align:middle line:84%
This graph is entirely done
with sales on the online site.

00:28:59.790 --> 00:29:03.180 align:middle line:84%
But what they graph is, this
is a state level analysis

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:05.790 align:middle line:90%
for, I think, 49 states.

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:07.260 align:middle line:84%
And what they put
on this is just

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:11.610 align:middle line:84%
a dot for every state of
what fraction of the shoes

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:14.670 align:middle line:84%
that are sold to
that state are boots

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:17.070 align:middle line:90%
and what fraction are sandals.

00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:20.830 align:middle line:84%
And what do you notice on
the right side of this graph?

00:29:20.830 --> 00:29:23.050 align:middle line:84%
We take the two warmest
US states, which

00:29:23.050 --> 00:29:25.190 align:middle line:90%
would be Hawaii and Florida.

00:29:25.190 --> 00:29:27.250 align:middle line:84%
And in Hawaii and
Florida, people

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:31.090 align:middle line:84%
don't buy a whole lot of boots
and they buy a lot of sandals.

00:29:31.090 --> 00:29:34.180 align:middle line:84%
I guess especially in Florida,
people buy a lot of sandals.

00:29:34.180 --> 00:29:36.820 align:middle line:84%
And then you go
down here and you

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:40.500 align:middle line:84%
look at what people are
buying in the coldest states.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:44.410 align:middle line:84%
So it's, I don't know if
that's North Dakota, Wyoming,

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:47.170 align:middle line:84%
Minnesota, places
like that, people buy

00:29:47.170 --> 00:29:49.030 align:middle line:90%
a lot of boots in North Dakota.

00:29:49.030 --> 00:29:53.230 align:middle line:84%
People buy fewer
sandals in North Dakota.

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:55.090 align:middle line:84%
Although I guess sort
of surprising things

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:56.040 align:middle line:90%
you learned from this.

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:57.498 align:middle line:84%
It turns out people
in North Dakota

00:29:57.498 --> 00:29:59.050 align:middle line:84%
and Wyoming actually
buy more sandals

00:29:59.050 --> 00:30:01.990 align:middle line:84%
than people in middle
temperature states.

00:30:01.990 --> 00:30:03.318 align:middle line:90%
I guess it may be--

00:30:03.318 --> 00:30:03.860 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:05.277 align:middle line:84%
Maybe if you live
in a cold place,

00:30:05.277 --> 00:30:08.335 align:middle line:84%
you go on vacation to Florida
and you need to buy sandals.

00:30:08.335 --> 00:30:10.210 align:middle line:84%
Or if you live in a
medium temperature state,

00:30:10.210 --> 00:30:11.920 align:middle line:84%
you just don't go
on as many vacations

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:13.370 align:middle line:90%
to warm weather states.

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:14.230 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:16.990 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, but their
thought from this graph

00:30:16.990 --> 00:30:18.550 align:middle line:84%
is if you look at
if this is what

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:20.590 align:middle line:90%
happens for online retailers.

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:22.690 align:middle line:90%
You imagine that you walk into--

00:30:22.690 --> 00:30:24.760 align:middle line:84%
even take a national
chain store,

00:30:24.760 --> 00:30:28.940 align:middle line:84%
you imagine that the national
chains in their North Dakota

00:30:28.940 --> 00:30:31.580 align:middle line:84%
stores, they have lots
of boots on the shelves,

00:30:31.580 --> 00:30:34.760 align:middle line:84%
and in their Florida stores,
they have very few winter

00:30:34.760 --> 00:30:36.530 align:middle line:90%
boots on the shelves.

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:38.370 align:middle line:84%
And so this would
just be an example.

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:42.440 align:middle line:84%
You know, this doesn't
prove that local stores

00:30:42.440 --> 00:30:47.650 align:middle line:84%
tailor their selections
to what customers want.

00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:50.330 align:middle line:84%
But this at least suggests
that local stores ought

00:30:50.330 --> 00:30:52.490 align:middle line:84%
to tailor their selections
to their customers,

00:30:52.490 --> 00:30:55.580 align:middle line:84%
and that ought to give
you back some of the gains

00:30:55.580 --> 00:30:58.130 align:middle line:84%
that we think is Amazon's gains
from product variety should

00:30:58.130 --> 00:31:00.670 align:middle line:84%
be gains from product
variety at these stores.

00:31:00.670 --> 00:31:04.210 align:middle line:90%


00:31:04.210 --> 00:31:10.430 align:middle line:84%
You know, the paper has a
second data set where they also

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:14.390 align:middle line:84%
got access to the
inventories of local stores

00:31:14.390 --> 00:31:19.400 align:middle line:84%
at Macy's and Payless Shoe
stores, which are just two chain

00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:21.270 align:middle line:90%
retail stores.

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:24.410 align:middle line:84%
And they find that there
is substantial differences

00:31:24.410 --> 00:31:27.883 align:middle line:84%
in the SKUs carried at
the different stores.

00:31:27.883 --> 00:31:29.300 align:middle line:84%
They don't really
have enough data

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:31.050 align:middle line:84%
to say whether it's
just completely random

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:36.333 align:middle line:84%
or how much of it is this store
carrying boots and this one

00:31:36.333 --> 00:31:38.750 align:middle line:84%
carrying sandals, or this one
having just different brands

00:31:38.750 --> 00:31:39.417 align:middle line:90%
from the others.

00:31:39.417 --> 00:31:42.860 align:middle line:84%
But what they find is that
especially at the Macy's, there

00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:45.380 align:middle line:84%
are almost no shoes that
all the Macy's have.

00:31:45.380 --> 00:31:47.855 align:middle line:84%
Different Macy's just
have different shoes.

00:31:47.855 --> 00:31:49.730 align:middle line:84%
There are a lot of them,
shoes, that are only

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:51.510 align:middle line:90%
found in one or two stores.

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:53.780 align:middle line:84%
Very few that are found
in 100% of stores.

00:31:53.780 --> 00:31:56.800 align:middle line:84%
At the Payless, there are
a lot of store shoes that

00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:59.300 align:middle line:84%
are found in every Payless and
a lot of shoes that are found

00:31:59.300 --> 00:32:00.776 align:middle line:90%
in like one or two Paylesses.

00:32:00.776 --> 00:32:04.280 align:middle line:90%


00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:08.210 align:middle line:84%
OK, anyway, so how
do they estimate?

00:32:08.210 --> 00:32:12.230 align:middle line:84%
So what they want to do is
estimate how important is

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:14.780 align:middle line:84%
this variation in
consumer preferences

00:32:14.780 --> 00:32:18.050 align:middle line:84%
and to estimate how
important if stores just

00:32:18.050 --> 00:32:20.900 align:middle line:84%
match their local
preferences, would stores

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:22.910 align:middle line:84%
be able to get most
of the benefits

00:32:22.910 --> 00:32:24.113 align:middle line:90%
that you get from Amazon?

00:32:24.113 --> 00:32:26.990 align:middle line:90%


00:32:26.990 --> 00:32:29.970 align:middle line:84%
So what they do is
demand estimation,

00:32:29.970 --> 00:32:33.720 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be a standard
kind of nested logit demand.

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:40.130 align:middle line:84%
So this is utility, customer I,
location L, buying product J.

00:32:40.130 --> 00:32:45.590 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be a mean utility
of product J in location L,

00:32:45.590 --> 00:32:50.160 align:middle line:84%
plus sort of nested logit
models, there's a shock.

00:32:50.160 --> 00:32:52.100 align:middle line:84%
You have this
idiosyncratic preference

00:32:52.100 --> 00:32:57.170 align:middle line:84%
of a customer for all
products in category J.

00:32:57.170 --> 00:32:58.700 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm looking
for snow boots,

00:32:58.700 --> 00:33:04.370 align:middle line:84%
I have a snow boot specific
shock, and then a weight.

00:33:04.370 --> 00:33:05.640 align:middle line:90%
I have two shocks.

00:33:05.640 --> 00:33:07.280 align:middle line:84%
One is the
category-specific shock,

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:09.990 align:middle line:84%
and then one is the
brand-specific shock.

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:12.330 align:middle line:84%
And they're going to estimate
this lambda parameter

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:14.163 align:middle line:84%
so they can tell whether
people's shocks are

00:33:14.163 --> 00:33:19.610 align:middle line:84%
mostly category-specific
or mostly product-specific.

00:33:19.610 --> 00:33:21.470 align:middle line:84%
And then what they're
going to assume

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:24.650 align:middle line:84%
is that the mean
utility provided

00:33:24.650 --> 00:33:28.310 align:middle line:84%
by product J in
location L is a function

00:33:28.310 --> 00:33:33.060 align:middle line:84%
of the product characteristics,
a function of the product price.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:37.520 align:middle line:84%
And then this other
xi sub LJ term,

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:42.440 align:middle line:84%
which that's the
idiosyncratic preference

00:33:42.440 --> 00:33:46.580 align:middle line:90%
for this product in location L.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:48.150 align:middle line:84%
They don't have the
temperature here.

00:33:48.150 --> 00:33:51.330 align:middle line:84%
So the people in Florida
don't like boots would be here

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:54.510 align:middle line:84%
or people in Hawaii like
sandals would be here.

00:33:54.510 --> 00:33:57.410 align:middle line:84%
But then you could
imagine there's also just

00:33:57.410 --> 00:33:59.660 align:middle line:84%
like these mayonnaise-like
preferences.

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:04.230 align:middle line:84%
Like, there's just UGGs boots
are popular in New England.

00:34:04.230 --> 00:34:06.320 align:middle line:84%
They're unfashionable
in California.

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:09.420 align:middle line:84%
Or this brand of
shoes is popular here,

00:34:09.420 --> 00:34:11.010 align:middle line:90%
it's not popular here.

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:14.014 align:middle line:84%
All of that would
go into the xi LJs.

00:34:14.014 --> 00:34:18.989 align:middle line:90%


00:34:18.989 --> 00:34:22.080 align:middle line:84%
A real challenge to
estimating demand

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:26.969 align:middle line:84%
is that most products,
you know, like there's

00:34:26.969 --> 00:34:30.900 align:middle line:84%
a very, very large number
of shoes in the world.

00:34:30.900 --> 00:34:35.790 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that most shoes
in most cities in most months

00:34:35.790 --> 00:34:38.179 align:middle line:90%
have zero sales.

00:34:38.179 --> 00:34:42.170 align:middle line:84%
And so dealing with quantity
data when you have zeros

00:34:42.170 --> 00:34:43.730 align:middle line:90%
is very difficult.

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:47.000 align:middle line:84%
If you remember, one
of the things we--

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:50.224 align:middle line:84%
standard approaches
people do in the load

00:34:50.224 --> 00:34:52.500 align:middle line:84%
to demand estimation
is you have,

00:34:52.500 --> 00:34:54.920 align:middle line:84%
you know, log of
the share of product

00:34:54.920 --> 00:34:58.730 align:middle line:84%
J minus log of the share
of the outside good

00:34:58.730 --> 00:35:03.655 align:middle line:84%
was X, J, beta minus Alpha,
P, J, something like that.

00:35:03.655 --> 00:35:07.100 align:middle line:90%


00:35:07.100 --> 00:35:10.040 align:middle line:84%
You convert to the market shares
to get back to the product

00:35:10.040 --> 00:35:12.530 align:middle line:84%
characteristics, and then
you can run IV regressions

00:35:12.530 --> 00:35:14.010 align:middle line:90%
to estimate demand.

00:35:14.010 --> 00:35:17.090 align:middle line:84%
You know, you can't do
these log transformations

00:35:17.090 --> 00:35:20.450 align:middle line:84%
when you have a whole bunch
of zeros in your data.

00:35:20.450 --> 00:35:23.030 align:middle line:84%
And so what do you do when you
have a whole bunch of zeros

00:35:23.030 --> 00:35:24.020 align:middle line:90%
in your data?

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:29.960 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, what they do in
this paper is discuss a GMM

00:35:29.960 --> 00:35:34.850 align:middle line:84%
approach to try to
deal with all the zeros

00:35:34.850 --> 00:35:36.600 align:middle line:84%
You also don't
want to throw out--

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:38.308 align:middle line:84%
another thing people
do is sometimes just

00:35:38.308 --> 00:35:39.430 align:middle line:90%
throw out all the zeros.

00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:41.790 align:middle line:84%
You clearly don't want to
throw out all the zeros

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:45.060 align:middle line:84%
because the goods that have
zero sales in one month

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:47.140 align:middle line:84%
will have positive
sales in the next month.

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:48.820 align:middle line:84%
They all have positive
mean utilities.

00:35:48.820 --> 00:35:50.550 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to
remove them and say,

00:35:50.550 --> 00:35:54.570 align:middle line:84%
no one wants to buy
from those goods.

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:59.280 align:middle line:84%
In particular, what they
note is that the number

00:35:59.280 --> 00:36:03.420 align:middle line:84%
of shoes that are sold
by this online website

00:36:03.420 --> 00:36:06.540 align:middle line:84%
in a particular city,
in a particular month

00:36:06.540 --> 00:36:11.240 align:middle line:84%
is smaller than the inventory
of a large shoe store.

00:36:11.240 --> 00:36:15.470 align:middle line:84%
So if you actually were to take
the realized shoe sales and say,

00:36:15.470 --> 00:36:17.120 align:middle line:84%
those people's
deltas are only based

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:19.850 align:middle line:84%
on the shoes that actually got
bought, what you'd conclude

00:36:19.850 --> 00:36:21.800 align:middle line:84%
is that a single physical
store could actually

00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:23.678 align:middle line:90%
supply the entire market.

00:36:23.678 --> 00:36:25.220 align:middle line:84%
And so it's just,
it's very important

00:36:25.220 --> 00:36:27.710 align:middle line:84%
to correctly deal with
all of these shoes that

00:36:27.710 --> 00:36:31.350 align:middle line:84%
are in the store and have
zero sales in a month.

00:36:31.350 --> 00:36:33.445 align:middle line:84%
But, you know,
people are buying.

00:36:33.445 --> 00:36:34.820 align:middle line:84%
You just have
these many products

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:37.710 align:middle line:84%
all with a fraction
of expected sales.

00:36:37.710 --> 00:36:39.213 align:middle line:84%
But those are what
people buy among.

00:36:39.213 --> 00:36:40.880 align:middle line:84%
And so they have to
deal with the zeros.

00:36:40.880 --> 00:36:44.470 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, one thing you'll
notice in the paper,

00:36:44.470 --> 00:36:47.170 align:middle line:84%
like, they spend a while talking
about how to deal with demand

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:49.288 align:middle line:84%
estimation when you
have lots of zeros.

00:36:49.288 --> 00:36:50.830 align:middle line:84%
If you ever have a
paper where you're

00:36:50.830 --> 00:36:54.700 align:middle line:84%
trying to do demand
estimation with lots of zeros,

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:56.802 align:middle line:84%
they discuss their
method and other methods

00:36:56.802 --> 00:36:58.510 align:middle line:84%
that people have used
to deal with zeros.

00:36:58.510 --> 00:37:01.210 align:middle line:84%
I think theirs is thought to be
a fairly good method for doing

00:37:01.210 --> 00:37:05.610 align:middle line:84%
demand estimation when you
have a whole bunch of zeros.

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:11.460 align:middle line:84%
But so anyway, the basic
idea is they aggregate sales

00:37:11.460 --> 00:37:13.150 align:middle line:90%
to the location month level.

00:37:13.150 --> 00:37:16.020 align:middle line:90%
They have 213 locations.

00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:22.150 align:middle line:84%
Most of the 213 locations
are metropolitan areas.

00:37:22.150 --> 00:37:27.120 align:middle line:84%
But then in addition, they
have each metropolitan area

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:28.950 align:middle line:84%
as a location
observation, and then they

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:31.680 align:middle line:84%
have the rest of the state
not in metropolitan areas

00:37:31.680 --> 00:37:33.390 align:middle line:90%
as an observation.

00:37:33.390 --> 00:37:36.780 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so they have
213 different locations.

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:41.040 align:middle line:84%
They do something that's
kind of like a BLP estimation

00:37:41.040 --> 00:37:43.000 align:middle line:90%
except dealing with the zeros.

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:45.870 align:middle line:90%
So they aggregate sales.

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:48.353 align:middle line:84%
They do something
very much like, I

00:37:48.353 --> 00:37:50.520 align:middle line:84%
don't know if you remember
back to Tobias's lecture,

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:53.520 align:middle line:84%
you're BLP instruments
for price are

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:55.500 align:middle line:84%
some function of the
characteristics of all

00:37:55.500 --> 00:37:57.130 align:middle line:90%
the competing brands.

00:37:57.130 --> 00:38:00.330 align:middle line:84%
So they have not
a very convincing

00:38:00.330 --> 00:38:03.070 align:middle line:84%
instrumentation strategy,
but that's what's available.

00:38:03.070 --> 00:38:06.520 align:middle line:84%
So they have a BLP style
instrumentation strategy

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:08.030 align:middle line:90%
dealing with the zeros.

00:38:08.030 --> 00:38:10.480 align:middle line:84%
And it's not a
pure cross section

00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:14.710 align:middle line:84%
because there's price variation,
because the prices vary

00:38:14.710 --> 00:38:15.560 align:middle line:90%
across months.

00:38:15.560 --> 00:38:18.550 align:middle line:84%
And you can see when this
shoe is on sale in some month,

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:20.620 align:middle line:90%
do sales go up.

00:38:20.620 --> 00:38:23.210 align:middle line:84%
Product reviews are
also time varying.

00:38:23.210 --> 00:38:25.330 align:middle line:84%
Shoes come out and they
have no reviews over time.

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:27.160 align:middle line:90%
They build up reviews.

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:30.290 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, higher reviews
are good for sales.

00:38:30.290 --> 00:38:34.090 align:middle line:84%
It turns out, though, also
zero reviews are good for sales

00:38:34.090 --> 00:38:36.820 align:middle line:84%
or positively associated
with sales as well.

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:40.240 align:middle line:84%
And this may be because
zero reviews means

00:38:40.240 --> 00:38:43.390 align:middle line:84%
it's a brand new product
that's just being introduced

00:38:43.390 --> 00:38:45.760 align:middle line:84%
with the latest fashion
or that's being advertised

00:38:45.760 --> 00:38:48.130 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:38:48.130 --> 00:38:52.210 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, and then the products,
that is also time varying.

00:38:52.210 --> 00:38:54.250 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, with all
of that, they're

00:38:54.250 --> 00:38:58.360 align:middle line:84%
able to estimate all
these coefficients.

00:38:58.360 --> 00:39:02.470 align:middle line:84%
And they can estimate a
distribution of the delta LJ's.

00:39:02.470 --> 00:39:06.280 align:middle line:84%
And the delta LJ's
indicate that there

00:39:06.280 --> 00:39:09.970 align:middle line:84%
is a lot of cross-market
heterogeneity in preferences.

00:39:09.970 --> 00:39:11.558 align:middle line:84%
And, you know, you
have to have faith

00:39:11.558 --> 00:39:13.600 align:middle line:84%
that they're dealing with
the zeros appropriately

00:39:13.600 --> 00:39:14.267 align:middle line:90%
to deal with it.

00:39:14.267 --> 00:39:17.410 align:middle line:84%
It's not just in some
markets these shoes actually

00:39:17.410 --> 00:39:20.133 align:middle line:84%
have sales, in some markets
these have actually have sales.

00:39:20.133 --> 00:39:22.300 align:middle line:84%
But I think they've done a
fairly good job of trying

00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:25.030 align:middle line:84%
to argue that, no,
there's underlying

00:39:25.030 --> 00:39:27.910 align:middle line:84%
cross sectional variation
in the propensities

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:30.350 align:middle line:84%
to purchase different
shoes in different places.

00:39:30.350 --> 00:39:32.080 align:middle line:84%
And again, I think
some of that is

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:34.700 align:middle line:84%
like the boots versus
sandals approach.

00:39:34.700 --> 00:39:39.110 align:middle line:84%
And some of that is just, you
know, shoes are a fashion good.

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:40.870 align:middle line:84%
And there are
brands of sneakers,

00:39:40.870 --> 00:39:43.360 align:middle line:84%
brands of shoes,
brands of whatever that

00:39:43.360 --> 00:39:45.980 align:middle line:84%
are more or less fashionable
in different cities.

00:39:45.980 --> 00:39:48.940 align:middle line:84%
For whatever reason, this has
become something people wear

00:39:48.940 --> 00:39:53.180 align:middle line:84%
and people are willing
to pay more for those.

00:39:53.180 --> 00:39:58.520 align:middle line:84%
OK, so how do they estimate
the gains from product variety?

00:39:58.520 --> 00:40:00.350 align:middle line:84%
Estimated gains from
product variety, this

00:40:00.350 --> 00:40:02.420 align:middle line:90%
is done with a counterfactual.

00:40:02.420 --> 00:40:06.470 align:middle line:84%
So they know all the
sales that Zappos,

00:40:06.470 --> 00:40:08.750 align:middle line:84%
or whatever the
retailer is, made.

00:40:08.750 --> 00:40:10.790 align:middle line:84%
And then they think about
a counterfactual world

00:40:10.790 --> 00:40:13.850 align:middle line:84%
where instead of choosing
from all the shoes at Zappos,

00:40:13.850 --> 00:40:16.910 align:middle line:84%
people could only choose
from a limited supply.

00:40:16.910 --> 00:40:18.720 align:middle line:84%
What do they do for
the limited supply?

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:21.260 align:middle line:84%
Well, the first
thing they do is they

00:40:21.260 --> 00:40:24.980 align:middle line:84%
use the data on the Macy's
and Payless Shoe Stores

00:40:24.980 --> 00:40:28.820 align:middle line:84%
to estimate the total
number of products

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:32.130 align:middle line:84%
available in each market as
a function of the population.

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:35.780 align:middle line:84%
So they would be doing, how
many different brands of shoes

00:40:35.780 --> 00:40:37.460 align:middle line:90%
are available in Boston?

00:40:37.460 --> 00:40:39.860 align:middle line:84%
Which you get by looking
at the overlap of all the

00:40:39.860 --> 00:40:42.140 align:middle line:84%
Macy's in Boston and the
overlap of the Macy's

00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:44.790 align:middle line:84%
in Boston with the Payless
Shoe Stores in Boston.

00:40:44.790 --> 00:40:46.700 align:middle line:84%
And then they have
census data knowing

00:40:46.700 --> 00:40:49.820 align:middle line:84%
how many shoe stores
there are in Boston

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:51.690 align:middle line:84%
and how large they
are in Boston.

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:55.350 align:middle line:84%
They assume that they have the
same overlap as they're seeing

00:40:55.350 --> 00:40:57.070 align:middle line:90%
in the Macy's and Paylesses.

00:40:57.070 --> 00:41:00.630 align:middle line:84%
And they're trying to estimate
how many total shoes are there

00:41:00.630 --> 00:41:05.423 align:middle line:84%
in the Boston area that you can
buy at physical retail stores.

00:41:05.423 --> 00:41:07.590 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, that's a bit of
an aggressive assumption,

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:09.690 align:middle line:84%
and that is assuming that
people could actually

00:41:09.690 --> 00:41:11.610 align:middle line:84%
know which local
Boston shoe store

00:41:11.610 --> 00:41:15.750 align:middle line:84%
to go to buy whatever particular
brand of boots they want.

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:19.830 align:middle line:84%
And then what they assume
is that offline consumers

00:41:19.830 --> 00:41:22.440 align:middle line:84%
have the choice
of the NL products

00:41:22.440 --> 00:41:26.910 align:middle line:84%
that have the highest delta
LJ's to choose from so

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:32.400 align:middle line:84%
that the physical stores
in each state or each city

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:34.780 align:middle line:90%
carry the most popular brands.

00:41:34.780 --> 00:41:38.580 align:middle line:84%
So again, it's kind of an
aggressive assumption in that,

00:41:38.580 --> 00:41:41.370 align:middle line:84%
you know, local stores are
tailoring their demand to what

00:41:41.370 --> 00:41:44.020 align:middle line:84%
people want and they're
tailoring it perfectly.

00:41:44.020 --> 00:41:45.790 align:middle line:84%
They're picking the
most popular brands.

00:41:45.790 --> 00:41:50.760 align:middle line:84%
And so, you know,
it's both we observe.

00:41:50.760 --> 00:41:55.050 align:middle line:84%
It's both we know where
to buy shoes physically,

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:58.290 align:middle line:84%
depending on what we want,
which store to go to,

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:00.960 align:middle line:84%
and the shoe stores are doing
perfect tailoring and carrying

00:42:00.960 --> 00:42:02.893 align:middle line:84%
the most popular shoes,
not making mistakes.

00:42:02.893 --> 00:42:05.490 align:middle line:90%


00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:09.900 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, so subject to that
caveat, as the paper suggests,

00:42:09.900 --> 00:42:12.110 align:middle line:84%
the online product
variety benefits

00:42:12.110 --> 00:42:16.080 align:middle line:84%
are much smaller than what
Brynjolfsson, Hu, and Smith

00:42:16.080 --> 00:42:16.870 align:middle line:90%
said.

00:42:16.870 --> 00:42:20.070 align:middle line:84%
And they're much smaller
in two different senses.

00:42:20.070 --> 00:42:24.690 align:middle line:84%
The first is that if you look at
the gain from product variety,

00:42:24.690 --> 00:42:29.010 align:middle line:84%
they argue that about half of
the gain from product variety

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:33.270 align:middle line:84%
would be provided
anyway by local stores

00:42:33.270 --> 00:42:36.510 align:middle line:84%
by just having heterogeneity
in local stores.

00:42:36.510 --> 00:42:39.146 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about
this sort of continuum.

00:42:39.146 --> 00:42:42.220 align:middle line:90%


00:42:42.220 --> 00:42:44.920 align:middle line:84%
Let me do the Brynjolfsson,
Hu, and Smith numbers.

00:42:44.920 --> 00:42:50.050 align:middle line:90%


00:42:50.050 --> 00:42:52.832 align:middle line:84%
So Brynjolfsson, Hu, and
Smith are all looking at sort

00:42:52.832 --> 00:42:57.670 align:middle line:84%
of, what's the consumer surplus
from there being one bookstore

00:42:57.670 --> 00:43:01.800 align:middle line:90%
that has 100,000 titles?

00:43:01.800 --> 00:43:08.750 align:middle line:84%
And then we have this sort of
Amazon with 2 million titles

00:43:08.750 --> 00:43:11.450 align:middle line:84%
and they're arguing
that this is 10.

00:43:11.450 --> 00:43:14.340 align:middle line:84%
This is like, if this
is 1, this is 10.

00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:15.733 align:middle line:90%
You get 10 times--

00:43:15.733 --> 00:43:17.650 align:middle line:84%
I'm putting the consumer
surplus in this axis.

00:43:17.650 --> 00:43:21.420 align:middle line:90%


00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:26.880 align:middle line:84%
And then what Quan and Williams
are saying is that we have--

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:28.845 align:middle line:90%
so this is--

00:43:28.845 --> 00:43:30.720 align:middle line:84%
Quan and Williams saying,
well, our benchmark

00:43:30.720 --> 00:43:37.080 align:middle line:84%
is going to be not one
bookstore, but all Boston

00:43:37.080 --> 00:43:39.150 align:middle line:90%
bookstores.

00:43:39.150 --> 00:43:41.220 align:middle line:84%
They don't know what all
Boston bookstores have,

00:43:41.220 --> 00:43:43.590 align:middle line:84%
but they just assume that
the Boston bookstores have

00:43:43.590 --> 00:43:46.290 align:middle line:84%
these non-overlapping
sets of books

00:43:46.290 --> 00:43:49.740 align:middle line:84%
and the non-overlapping sets of
books tailored to what the MIT

00:43:49.740 --> 00:43:53.070 align:middle line:84%
students want, tailored to what
everybody else in Boston wants.

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:57.780 align:middle line:84%
And what they find
is that, you know--

00:43:57.780 --> 00:44:00.600 align:middle line:84%
well, they do all shoe
stores, but all shoe stores

00:44:00.600 --> 00:44:02.800 align:middle line:84%
thing compared to
the one shoe store,

00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:06.690 align:middle line:84%
they're arguing that all shoe
stores get you about half

00:44:06.690 --> 00:44:08.800 align:middle line:84%
of the way to the
Amazon rankings.

00:44:08.800 --> 00:44:10.890 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense that
Quan and Williams

00:44:10.890 --> 00:44:18.980 align:middle line:84%
may be overstating the
gains by 100% or something.

00:44:18.980 --> 00:44:20.757 align:middle line:90%
And so that's one point.

00:44:20.757 --> 00:44:22.340 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
point of the paper

00:44:22.340 --> 00:44:25.165 align:middle line:84%
that is not emphasized
as much in just

00:44:25.165 --> 00:44:26.540 align:middle line:84%
in the title and
the introduction

00:44:26.540 --> 00:44:34.310 align:middle line:84%
is that they also find that
the gains from product variety

00:44:34.310 --> 00:44:39.080 align:middle line:84%
are just much smaller than
are reported elsewhere.

00:44:39.080 --> 00:44:49.950 align:middle line:84%
So that whereas
Brynjolfsson, Hu, and Smith

00:44:49.950 --> 00:44:52.500 align:middle line:84%
are saying that these
gains are 10 times the gain

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:55.020 align:middle line:90%
from a 5% price cut.

00:44:55.020 --> 00:44:58.770 align:middle line:84%
What they find is that the
gains in the shoe store data

00:44:58.770 --> 00:45:06.060 align:middle line:84%
look like they're, you know,
2 times the benefit of a--

00:45:06.060 --> 00:45:07.200 align:middle line:90%
so anyway, sorry.

00:45:07.200 --> 00:45:10.530 align:middle line:90%


00:45:10.530 --> 00:45:12.450 align:middle line:90%
If I make this 1, this is 2.

00:45:12.450 --> 00:45:15.270 align:middle line:84%
So the gain is just
equivalent to a 5.

00:45:15.270 --> 00:45:19.710 align:middle line:84%
The gain is like equivalent
to a 5% price cut.

00:45:19.710 --> 00:45:23.440 align:middle line:84%
So it's like this number is
here, this number is here.

00:45:23.440 --> 00:45:25.460 align:middle line:84%
So what they're
arguing is that the--

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:27.900 align:middle line:90%
actually, no, I take that back.

00:45:27.900 --> 00:45:29.970 align:middle line:84%
It's this one
compared to this one

00:45:29.970 --> 00:45:33.480 align:middle line:84%
I think is roughly
equivalent to a 5% price cut.

00:45:33.480 --> 00:45:36.930 align:middle line:84%
So what they're finding is that
the gains from product variety

00:45:36.930 --> 00:45:39.360 align:middle line:84%
are just much, much
smaller in shoes

00:45:39.360 --> 00:45:42.400 align:middle line:84%
than Brynjolfsson, Hu, and
Smith said they were in books.

00:45:42.400 --> 00:45:45.840 align:middle line:84%
And because the gains
are so much smaller,

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:49.770 align:middle line:84%
and you take out this thing
is halfway there already, what

00:45:49.770 --> 00:45:52.160 align:middle line:84%
you're getting is the
product variety gains

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:56.120 align:middle line:84%
are really only like
equivalent to a 5% price cut.

00:45:56.120 --> 00:45:59.360 align:middle line:90%


00:45:59.360 --> 00:46:03.590 align:middle line:84%
So it's saying, at
least in the shoe sense,

00:46:03.590 --> 00:46:06.620 align:middle line:84%
product variety
gains are, you know,

00:46:06.620 --> 00:46:08.555 align:middle line:84%
10 times smaller
than you would have

00:46:08.555 --> 00:46:10.430 align:middle line:84%
might have thought from
the Brynjolfsson, Hu,

00:46:10.430 --> 00:46:11.630 align:middle line:90%
and Smith paper.

00:46:11.630 --> 00:46:14.510 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, you know,
is that because shoes

00:46:14.510 --> 00:46:16.360 align:middle line:90%
are very different from books?

00:46:16.360 --> 00:46:17.730 align:middle line:90%
That's one possibility.

00:46:17.730 --> 00:46:20.253 align:middle line:84%
You know, like, every book
is very, very different

00:46:20.253 --> 00:46:21.170 align:middle line:90%
from every other book.

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:23.990 align:middle line:84%
You could imagine, once
you've got a million shoes,

00:46:23.990 --> 00:46:26.150 align:middle line:84%
the ability to make
a million first shoe

00:46:26.150 --> 00:46:29.070 align:middle line:84%
that's very different from all
existing shoes is very hard.

00:46:29.070 --> 00:46:31.430 align:middle line:84%
Whereas every new book is
just completely different

00:46:31.430 --> 00:46:32.940 align:middle line:90%
text from every other book.

00:46:32.940 --> 00:46:34.820 align:middle line:84%
There may be more
inherent variety in books

00:46:34.820 --> 00:46:37.160 align:middle line:84%
than there are in
shoes, but I think

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:41.300 align:middle line:84%
it may also be that they
may have done a better

00:46:41.300 --> 00:46:44.810 align:middle line:84%
job of dealing with
estimating what's

00:46:44.810 --> 00:46:47.790 align:middle line:84%
true product variety versus
spurious product variety.

00:46:47.790 --> 00:46:50.313 align:middle line:90%
You know, like the--

00:46:50.313 --> 00:46:51.230 align:middle line:90%
I don't if I remember.

00:46:51.230 --> 00:46:52.880 align:middle line:84%
Like, I've discussed,
like, when you're

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:55.220 align:middle line:84%
trying to estimate
consumer variety in demand,

00:46:55.220 --> 00:46:57.020 align:middle line:84%
like, consumer
welfare from demand

00:46:57.020 --> 00:46:59.010 align:middle line:84%
and you have
heterogeneous products,

00:46:59.010 --> 00:47:04.580 align:middle line:84%
you know, everyone's always
doing this X, J, Beta

00:47:04.580 --> 00:47:09.142 align:middle line:84%
minus alpha, P, J
plus Epsilon, IJ.

00:47:09.142 --> 00:47:10.850 align:middle line:84%
And they're always
assuming these Epsilon

00:47:10.850 --> 00:47:14.360 align:middle line:84%
IJ's are logit distributed,
which has a fairly thick tail,

00:47:14.360 --> 00:47:16.280 align:middle line:84%
and that every Epsilon
IJ is independent

00:47:16.280 --> 00:47:19.400 align:middle line:90%
of every other Epsilon IJ.

00:47:19.400 --> 00:47:22.370 align:middle line:84%
Again, once you get to the
millionth different shoe,

00:47:22.370 --> 00:47:25.010 align:middle line:84%
is it really an Epsilon IJ
that's completely independent

00:47:25.010 --> 00:47:27.420 align:middle line:84%
of your taste for every
other shoe out there?

00:47:27.420 --> 00:47:29.180 align:middle line:84%
It's highly likely
those Epsilons are

00:47:29.180 --> 00:47:31.340 align:middle line:90%
highly correlated across shoes.

00:47:31.340 --> 00:47:32.900 align:middle line:90%
At least by having the--

00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:37.310 align:middle line:84%
you know, they've got those
category-specific shocks

00:47:37.310 --> 00:47:39.200 align:middle line:84%
by putting in the
category-specific shocks

00:47:39.200 --> 00:47:42.680 align:middle line:84%
and assuming the magnitude and
doing a careful job of thinking

00:47:42.680 --> 00:47:45.320 align:middle line:84%
about the zeros,
they may be avoiding

00:47:45.320 --> 00:47:48.170 align:middle line:84%
overstating how big all
these Epsilon IJ's are

00:47:48.170 --> 00:47:50.490 align:middle line:84%
and all this surplus
we're getting from all

00:47:50.490 --> 00:47:53.840 align:middle line:90%
these new different products.

00:47:53.840 --> 00:47:55.490 align:middle line:84%
And I guess one
other paper I thought

00:47:55.490 --> 00:47:57.115 align:middle line:84%
I'd mention-- one
other thing I thought

00:47:57.115 --> 00:48:00.110 align:middle line:84%
I'd mentioned in the
paper is that they

00:48:00.110 --> 00:48:02.240 align:middle line:84%
know that Macy's and Payless
carry different shoes

00:48:02.240 --> 00:48:03.140 align:middle line:90%
in different stores.

00:48:03.140 --> 00:48:06.320 align:middle line:84%
They ask, how important
is it for stores

00:48:06.320 --> 00:48:08.160 align:middle line:90%
to tailor their selections?

00:48:08.160 --> 00:48:09.680 align:middle line:84%
And what they find
is that there's

00:48:09.680 --> 00:48:11.010 align:middle line:90%
a gain that firms should do it.

00:48:11.010 --> 00:48:12.180 align:middle line:90%
It's not that large.

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:16.550 align:middle line:84%
The gain from customizing stores
is estimated to be about 6%

00:48:16.550 --> 00:48:17.240 align:middle line:90%
in revenue.

00:48:17.240 --> 00:48:19.520 align:middle line:84%
So you could just carry
all the standard shoes

00:48:19.520 --> 00:48:21.830 align:middle line:84%
or you could tailor the
demand in your city.

00:48:21.830 --> 00:48:25.370 align:middle line:84%
You tailor the demand in your
city, you get 6% more profit.

00:48:25.370 --> 00:48:27.600 align:middle line:84%
So it seems like
it's worth doing.

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:29.600 align:middle line:84%
Firms should want to do
this, especially if they

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:30.990 align:middle line:90%
have thin profit margins.

00:48:30.990 --> 00:48:37.250 align:middle line:84%
But it's boundedly-- tailoring
is boundedly profitable?

00:48:37.250 --> 00:48:43.920 align:middle line:90%


00:48:43.920 --> 00:48:44.780 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:48:44.780 --> 00:48:46.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Was this
paper actually

00:48:46.560 --> 00:48:47.800 align:middle line:90%
response to names on paper?

00:48:47.800 --> 00:48:49.543 align:middle line:84%
Or was it just
kind of disjointed?

00:48:49.543 --> 00:48:51.460 align:middle line:84%
Because couldn't you
just do the same exercise

00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:55.075 align:middle line:84%
they did in the Amazon paper but
with shoes [INAUDIBLE] exchange?

00:48:55.075 --> 00:48:57.200 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Yeah, so
certainly they could have--

00:48:57.200 --> 00:49:00.580 align:middle line:90%


00:49:00.580 --> 00:49:02.530 align:middle line:84%
you know, so they
actually have--

00:49:02.530 --> 00:49:03.730 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:49:03.730 --> 00:49:06.080 align:middle line:84%
They do several different
calculations in the paper.

00:49:06.080 --> 00:49:07.910 align:middle line:84%
Like, suppose we
estimate demand this way.

00:49:07.910 --> 00:49:09.410 align:middle line:84%
Suppose we estimate
demand this way.

00:49:09.410 --> 00:49:11.230 align:middle line:84%
Suppose we estimate
demand this way.

00:49:11.230 --> 00:49:13.810 align:middle line:84%
You know, in some sense
doing demand worse and worse

00:49:13.810 --> 00:49:16.460 align:middle line:84%
and worse, doing worse and
worse demand estimation.

00:49:16.460 --> 00:49:19.430 align:middle line:84%
And they do present
several things.

00:49:19.430 --> 00:49:21.250 align:middle line:84%
If you've done this
estimate, instead you'd

00:49:21.250 --> 00:49:23.050 align:middle line:84%
get numbers that are
two times as big.

00:49:23.050 --> 00:49:24.467 align:middle line:84%
If you do these
estimates instead,

00:49:24.467 --> 00:49:26.240 align:middle line:84%
you get things that
are five times as big.

00:49:26.240 --> 00:49:29.020 align:middle line:90%
So they do get--

00:49:29.020 --> 00:49:33.873 align:middle line:84%
you know, it's not exactly the
same calculation as the Amazon

00:49:33.873 --> 00:49:35.290 align:middle line:84%
data, but they do
something that's

00:49:35.290 --> 00:49:39.370 align:middle line:84%
more like the naive calculations
where you treat everything

00:49:39.370 --> 00:49:42.100 align:middle line:84%
as national market shares
and use logit models

00:49:42.100 --> 00:49:44.080 align:middle line:84%
and show that you can
get things many times

00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:45.788 align:middle line:84%
bigger than the
estimates they've gotten.

00:49:45.788 --> 00:49:52.490 align:middle line:90%


00:49:52.490 --> 00:49:55.313 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I think these are the
things that obviously you

00:49:55.313 --> 00:49:56.980 align:middle line:84%
couldn't have done
with the Amazon data.

00:49:56.980 --> 00:49:59.630 align:middle line:84%
Like, the Brynjolfsson,
Hu, and Smith

00:49:59.630 --> 00:50:02.420 align:middle line:84%
didn't have anything about
what is in local bookstores

00:50:02.420 --> 00:50:05.120 align:middle line:84%
and how much overlap there
is across local bookstores

00:50:05.120 --> 00:50:06.115 align:middle line:90%
and stuff like that.

00:50:06.115 --> 00:50:13.270 align:middle line:90%


00:50:13.270 --> 00:50:16.660 align:middle line:84%
But certainly, the paper is
very much written in response

00:50:16.660 --> 00:50:19.600 align:middle line:84%
to literature saying there
are these really big private--

00:50:19.600 --> 00:50:21.980 align:middle line:84%
really big product
variety benefits.

00:50:21.980 --> 00:50:29.260 align:middle line:84%
And I think the paper is more
about the benefits are smaller

00:50:29.260 --> 00:50:31.780 align:middle line:84%
because of the variety in
retail than the benefits are

00:50:31.780 --> 00:50:34.040 align:middle line:84%
smaller if you just do the
econometrics correctly.

00:50:34.040 --> 00:50:35.295 align:middle line:90%
But I think it is about both.

00:50:35.295 --> 00:50:38.690 align:middle line:90%


00:50:38.690 --> 00:50:39.514 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:50:39.514 --> 00:50:42.500 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, final
paper I want to cover.

00:50:42.500 --> 00:50:46.430 align:middle line:84%
This is a working paper
that Sara and I have,

00:50:46.430 --> 00:50:49.700 align:middle line:84%
which I guess has two
different motivations.

00:50:49.700 --> 00:50:53.570 align:middle line:84%
You know, one is also this is a
paper about trying to estimate

00:50:53.570 --> 00:50:56.240 align:middle line:84%
how big product
variety benefits are

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:58.940 align:middle line:84%
and how big product variety
benefits are in an area where

00:50:58.940 --> 00:51:01.460 align:middle line:84%
we think the product variety
benefits would be bigger,

00:51:01.460 --> 00:51:03.000 align:middle line:90%
which is in used books.

00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:06.800 align:middle line:84%
And then the other is also
sometimes you can say it is a,

00:51:06.800 --> 00:51:12.260 align:middle line:84%
you know, Chicago school
critique of our search

00:51:12.260 --> 00:51:13.610 align:middle line:90%
and obfuscation paper.

00:51:13.610 --> 00:51:15.690 align:middle line:84%
Where in the search
and obfuscation paper

00:51:15.690 --> 00:51:20.030 align:middle line:84%
we argued that you see
these not super low

00:51:20.030 --> 00:51:22.370 align:middle line:84%
and dispersed prices
for computer components

00:51:22.370 --> 00:51:27.830 align:middle line:84%
because the firms
have an incentive

00:51:27.830 --> 00:51:29.900 align:middle line:84%
to obfuscate what
they're selling the price

00:51:29.900 --> 00:51:32.930 align:middle line:84%
is at and invent these add-ons
or invent complicated things

00:51:32.930 --> 00:51:37.370 align:middle line:84%
that raise search costs and
keep prices high and disperses

00:51:37.370 --> 00:51:39.110 align:middle line:84%
as they are in a
search cost model

00:51:39.110 --> 00:51:42.470 align:middle line:84%
rather than letting perfect
competition come out here.

00:51:42.470 --> 00:51:47.090 align:middle line:84%
This paper is going to provide
an alternate explanation for why

00:51:47.090 --> 00:51:48.810 align:middle line:90%
prices can be high in dispersed.

00:51:48.810 --> 00:51:51.110 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be
that in some sense,

00:51:51.110 --> 00:51:53.490 align:middle line:84%
prices are high and dispersed
for two different reasons.

00:51:53.490 --> 00:51:56.150 align:middle line:84%
One is prices are
high on the internet

00:51:56.150 --> 00:51:59.700 align:middle line:84%
because the internet is so good
at matching products to people.

00:51:59.700 --> 00:52:06.770 align:middle line:84%
And if you think about a used
book that you had as a child.

00:52:06.770 --> 00:52:10.700 align:middle line:84%
You know, and then you have
a used book as a child,

00:52:10.700 --> 00:52:12.090 align:middle line:90%
you now have a niece or nephew.

00:52:12.090 --> 00:52:16.310 align:middle line:84%
You want to buy them the same
picture book that you remember

00:52:16.310 --> 00:52:17.640 align:middle line:90%
from when you were a kid.

00:52:17.640 --> 00:52:20.170 align:middle line:84%
You're willing to pay many
dollars for that book.

00:52:20.170 --> 00:52:21.920 align:middle line:84%
There are a million
other people out there

00:52:21.920 --> 00:52:23.820 align:middle line:84%
who didn't remember that
book when they were kids.

00:52:23.820 --> 00:52:26.445 align:middle line:84%
There are probably hundreds of
copies of it sitting in physical

00:52:26.445 --> 00:52:29.210 align:middle line:84%
used bookstores with $0.10
marked inside the cover that no

00:52:29.210 --> 00:52:30.510 align:middle line:90%
one is ever buying.

00:52:30.510 --> 00:52:32.930 align:middle line:84%
If we get those books in the
hands of the right people,

00:52:32.930 --> 00:52:34.950 align:middle line:84%
the willingness to pay
is way, way higher.

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:39.170 align:middle line:84%
In fact, actually, we say the
paper started when Sara was

00:52:39.170 --> 00:52:41.300 align:middle line:84%
writing a paper on
pharmaceuticals and needed some

00:52:41.300 --> 00:52:46.400 align:middle line:84%
obscure pharmaceutical book that
someone had written in the 1970s

00:52:46.400 --> 00:52:48.770 align:middle line:84%
that the MIT libraries
didn't carry.

00:52:48.770 --> 00:52:53.280 align:middle line:84%
You know, websites selling
used books online were new.

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:56.690 align:middle line:84%
She actually went on to
websites selling used books,

00:52:56.690 --> 00:52:59.750 align:middle line:84%
found the book, and it's like,
wow, I can find this book.

00:52:59.750 --> 00:53:01.320 align:middle line:90%
I've been trying to find this.

00:53:01.320 --> 00:53:02.233 align:middle line:90%
It's only $15.

00:53:02.233 --> 00:53:03.900 align:middle line:84%
I can pay it out of
my research account.

00:53:03.900 --> 00:53:06.140 align:middle line:84%
I'm thrilled to get it
for $15 instead of $100.

00:53:06.140 --> 00:53:09.000 align:middle line:84%
Gets it and then got
the book in like $0.25

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:11.140 align:middle line:84%
was penciled inside
the front cover of it.

00:53:11.140 --> 00:53:14.250 align:middle line:84%
And she realized it had been
sitting in a bookstore in Iowa

00:53:14.250 --> 00:53:16.500 align:middle line:84%
City since some economics
professor retired

00:53:16.500 --> 00:53:19.570 align:middle line:84%
for the last decade, and no one
wanted to pay $0.25 for this.

00:53:19.570 --> 00:53:21.750 align:middle line:84%
And she was thrilled
to get it for $15

00:53:21.750 --> 00:53:24.480 align:middle line:84%
and realizing that this
is potential huge increase

00:53:24.480 --> 00:53:27.162 align:middle line:84%
in social welfare
of match quality.

00:53:27.162 --> 00:53:28.620 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so part of
what we're doing

00:53:28.620 --> 00:53:31.423 align:middle line:84%
is underestimate
that match quality.

00:53:31.423 --> 00:53:33.090 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
thought in the paper

00:53:33.090 --> 00:53:36.840 align:middle line:84%
is that, it's nice to explain
two things with one story.

00:53:36.840 --> 00:53:38.520 align:middle line:84%
Like, prices are
high and dispersed

00:53:38.520 --> 00:53:39.520 align:middle line:90%
because of search costs.

00:53:39.520 --> 00:53:41.320 align:middle line:84%
But we think there
are two stories here.

00:53:41.320 --> 00:53:43.530 align:middle line:84%
Prices are high because
of match quality.

00:53:43.530 --> 00:53:46.912 align:middle line:84%
And prices are dispersed
because some websites are better

00:53:46.912 --> 00:53:48.870 align:middle line:84%
at selling things than
others, and the websites

00:53:48.870 --> 00:53:49.860 align:middle line:84%
that are better
at selling things

00:53:49.860 --> 00:53:52.470 align:middle line:84%
are able to command much higher
prices than the ones that

00:53:52.470 --> 00:53:54.660 align:middle line:90%
have limited attention.

00:53:54.660 --> 00:53:56.830 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so what
does the paper do?

00:53:56.830 --> 00:53:58.940 align:middle line:90%
It's a three-part paper.

00:53:58.940 --> 00:54:02.910 align:middle line:84%
First, it's developing simple
models to basically illustrate

00:54:02.910 --> 00:54:04.623 align:middle line:90%
the ideas.

00:54:04.623 --> 00:54:06.290 align:middle line:84%
You know, one and two
are really, again,

00:54:06.290 --> 00:54:09.920 align:middle line:84%
just me always aspiring to
be like Hendrix and Porter

00:54:09.920 --> 00:54:12.295 align:middle line:84%
and having this theory, having
predictions of the theory,

00:54:12.295 --> 00:54:14.503 align:middle line:84%
testing the predictions of
the theory to convince you

00:54:14.503 --> 00:54:15.360 align:middle line:90%
that it's right.

00:54:15.360 --> 00:54:16.880 align:middle line:84%
And then the back
part of the paper

00:54:16.880 --> 00:54:20.300 align:middle line:84%
is structural estimates to try
to say how big are the welfare

00:54:20.300 --> 00:54:24.860 align:middle line:84%
gains from improved
accessibility of used books sold

00:54:24.860 --> 00:54:28.050 align:middle line:84%
online versus in
physical stores.

00:54:28.050 --> 00:54:30.490 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:54:30.490 --> 00:54:34.080 align:middle line:84%
So first, let me start
with the basic theory.

00:54:34.080 --> 00:54:38.900 align:middle line:84%
And this is a simple model
of dynamic monopoly pricing

00:54:38.900 --> 00:54:40.490 align:middle line:90%
of a variety that we don't--

00:54:40.490 --> 00:54:43.580 align:middle line:84%
I guess I never covered
in the IO class.

00:54:43.580 --> 00:54:45.800 align:middle line:84%
But so imagine that
you have a seller

00:54:45.800 --> 00:54:48.090 align:middle line:84%
and the seller just
has one unit to sell.

00:54:48.090 --> 00:54:49.350 align:middle line:90%
There's no production here.

00:54:49.350 --> 00:54:51.180 align:middle line:90%
So this is a unique item.

00:54:51.180 --> 00:54:52.760 align:middle line:90%
You're just trying to sell it.

00:54:52.760 --> 00:54:54.620 align:middle line:84%
And then you have
customers who have

00:54:54.620 --> 00:54:58.040 align:middle line:84%
values VJ drawn from
some distribution F

00:54:58.040 --> 00:55:00.980 align:middle line:84%
and they just arrive at
some Poisson rate gamma.

00:55:00.980 --> 00:55:02.780 align:middle line:90%
They walk into your store.

00:55:02.780 --> 00:55:05.000 align:middle line:84%
When they walk into
your store and are

00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:06.950 align:middle line:84%
interested in this
pharmaceutical textbook,

00:55:06.950 --> 00:55:10.260 align:middle line:84%
you have to either sell it to
them immediately or not at all.

00:55:10.260 --> 00:55:13.007 align:middle line:84%
Because if they walk into
your store, they see the book,

00:55:13.007 --> 00:55:15.090 align:middle line:84%
they look at the price,
they don't want to buy it,

00:55:15.090 --> 00:55:15.780 align:middle line:90%
they walk out.

00:55:15.780 --> 00:55:16.947 align:middle line:90%
You'll never see them again.

00:55:16.947 --> 00:55:19.310 align:middle line:90%


00:55:19.310 --> 00:55:21.610 align:middle line:90%
How should you set your prices?

00:55:21.610 --> 00:55:24.800 align:middle line:84%
Well, you know, the
demand from these

00:55:24.800 --> 00:55:26.610 align:middle line:84%
are one-time consumers
who are myopic.

00:55:26.610 --> 00:55:28.460 align:middle line:84%
So the demand at
price P is just 1

00:55:28.460 --> 00:55:30.510 align:middle line:84%
minus capital F of
P, the probability

00:55:30.510 --> 00:55:33.420 align:middle line:84%
that the consumer's
value is above P.

00:55:33.420 --> 00:55:37.317 align:middle line:84%
So the expected profit
is the expectation

00:55:37.317 --> 00:55:39.150 align:middle line:84%
of setting a price of
P and leaving it there

00:55:39.150 --> 00:55:42.600 align:middle line:84%
is the expectation of pe
to the minus rt, where

00:55:42.600 --> 00:55:48.000 align:middle line:84%
t is the random time at
which you will sell the book.

00:55:48.000 --> 00:55:49.530 align:middle line:84%
In this model, as
long as you set

00:55:49.530 --> 00:55:51.865 align:middle line:84%
any p that's less than
the upper bound of F,

00:55:51.865 --> 00:55:53.490 align:middle line:84%
you'll always sell
the book eventually,

00:55:53.490 --> 00:55:55.800 align:middle line:84%
you just may sell it a
million years in the future

00:55:55.800 --> 00:55:58.980 align:middle line:84%
and the [INAUDIBLE] rt will
eat up all your profits.

00:55:58.980 --> 00:56:03.150 align:middle line:84%
OK, so what we're going
to do is integrate pe

00:56:03.150 --> 00:56:06.960 align:middle line:84%
to the minus rt
times the density

00:56:06.960 --> 00:56:10.440 align:middle line:84%
of the probability
of selling the book

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:12.760 align:middle line:90%
in the interval tt plus dt.

00:56:12.760 --> 00:56:16.100 align:middle line:90%


00:56:16.100 --> 00:56:18.720 align:middle line:84%
Gamma is the Poisson
arrival rate.

00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:22.430 align:middle line:84%
Gamma D of P is the
Poisson arrival rate

00:56:22.430 --> 00:56:26.240 align:middle line:84%
of people willing to
pay at least P. And so

00:56:26.240 --> 00:56:28.280 align:middle line:84%
the density of the
time you sell it

00:56:28.280 --> 00:56:34.033 align:middle line:84%
is gamma D of pe to the
minus gamma D of pt.

00:56:34.033 --> 00:56:36.200 align:middle line:84%
You may have seen like if
you have a Poisson arrival

00:56:36.200 --> 00:56:40.460 align:middle line:84%
rate, gamma, you know,
the density of arrivals

00:56:40.460 --> 00:56:45.950 align:middle line:90%
is gamma e to the minus gamma t.

00:56:45.950 --> 00:56:49.860 align:middle line:84%
And so the arrival rate of
people willing to pay p is this.

00:56:49.860 --> 00:56:54.390 align:middle line:84%
So this is the density of
the arrival distribution.

00:56:54.390 --> 00:56:57.330 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out this is a
nice, well-behaved integral.

00:56:57.330 --> 00:57:03.330 align:middle line:84%
And so the price is a function
of p as this expression.

00:57:03.330 --> 00:57:08.310 align:middle line:84%
It's p times gamma D of p
over r plus gamma D of p.

00:57:08.310 --> 00:57:13.120 align:middle line:84%
And so as gamma
D of p goes to 1,

00:57:13.120 --> 00:57:17.410 align:middle line:84%
you're selling it almost
instantly and your profit is p.

00:57:17.410 --> 00:57:21.595 align:middle line:84%
As gamma D of p goes
to zero, the r over

00:57:21.595 --> 00:57:23.470 align:middle line:84%
outweighs the gamma D
of p, and you basically

00:57:23.470 --> 00:57:26.500 align:middle line:84%
are getting something that's
going towards zero because this

00:57:26.500 --> 00:57:31.210 align:middle line:84%
is the discounting and this is
the price at which you sell it.

00:57:31.210 --> 00:57:35.030 align:middle line:84%
And so we can think of monopoly
pricing in two different ways.

00:57:35.030 --> 00:57:37.540 align:middle line:84%
One way to think about it
is just the monopoly price

00:57:37.540 --> 00:57:41.140 align:middle line:84%
is the maximizer
of this expression.

00:57:41.140 --> 00:57:43.750 align:middle line:84%
Second way to think about it
is like a dynamic programming

00:57:43.750 --> 00:57:44.980 align:middle line:90%
approach.

00:57:44.980 --> 00:57:47.085 align:middle line:84%
This good doesn't have
any production costs,

00:57:47.085 --> 00:57:49.210 align:middle line:84%
but it has an opportunity
cost because you can only

00:57:49.210 --> 00:57:50.500 align:middle line:90%
sell it once.

00:57:50.500 --> 00:57:55.570 align:middle line:84%
And so if pi star is your
equilibrium expected profit

00:57:55.570 --> 00:57:58.060 align:middle line:84%
from holding one
unit of this good,

00:57:58.060 --> 00:58:00.940 align:middle line:84%
you're thinking about someone's
in my store now, what price

00:58:00.940 --> 00:58:03.250 align:middle line:90%
should I quote them?

00:58:03.250 --> 00:58:07.060 align:middle line:84%
What I want to do is maximize
p minus pi star D of p.

00:58:07.060 --> 00:58:09.820 align:middle line:84%
I'm maximizing price
minus opportunity cost

00:58:09.820 --> 00:58:10.910 align:middle line:90%
times the demand.

00:58:10.910 --> 00:58:12.410 align:middle line:84%
And so another way
to think about it

00:58:12.410 --> 00:58:19.610 align:middle line:84%
is that pm has to solve this
where pi star is pi of pm.

00:58:19.610 --> 00:58:21.350 align:middle line:84%
So it's two different
ways to think

00:58:21.350 --> 00:58:23.843 align:middle line:84%
about monopoly pricing
for a unique item

00:58:23.843 --> 00:58:25.260 align:middle line:84%
when you have a
customer arrivals.

00:58:25.260 --> 00:58:29.030 align:middle line:90%


00:58:29.030 --> 00:58:32.360 align:middle line:84%
Observations from this
monopoly pricing model.

00:58:32.360 --> 00:58:36.795 align:middle line:84%
Monopoly prices increasing
in the customer arrival rate.

00:58:36.795 --> 00:58:38.670 align:middle line:84%
Why is the increase in
customer arrival rate?

00:58:38.670 --> 00:58:40.670 align:middle line:84%
Because the higher is the
customer arrival rate,

00:58:40.670 --> 00:58:42.620 align:middle line:84%
the higher is your
opportunity cost.

00:58:42.620 --> 00:58:47.040 align:middle line:84%
This is, again, a 14.121
standard exam question.

00:58:47.040 --> 00:58:49.040 align:middle line:84%
Your monopoly
price is increasing

00:58:49.040 --> 00:58:52.850 align:middle line:84%
in your cost, full stop
because of this function

00:58:52.850 --> 00:58:54.420 align:middle line:90%
as increasing differences.

00:58:54.420 --> 00:58:59.930 align:middle line:84%
So monopoly price is increasing
opportunity cost, therefore

00:58:59.930 --> 00:59:02.240 align:middle line:84%
dynamic monopoly price
increases in arrival rate

00:59:02.240 --> 00:59:04.760 align:middle line:84%
because arrival rate increases
your opportunity set.

00:59:04.760 --> 00:59:07.370 align:middle line:90%
Opportunity cost goes up.

00:59:07.370 --> 00:59:10.640 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, if consumers start
to arrive at higher rates

00:59:10.640 --> 00:59:12.560 align:middle line:84%
online than they did
in physical stores,

00:59:12.560 --> 00:59:14.340 align:middle line:84%
you're going to
get higher prices.

00:59:14.340 --> 00:59:16.430 align:middle line:84%
And if there's a rival
rate heterogeneity

00:59:16.430 --> 00:59:18.710 align:middle line:84%
across online
stores, that's going

00:59:18.710 --> 00:59:20.010 align:middle line:90%
to lead to price dispersion.

00:59:20.010 --> 00:59:22.545 align:middle line:84%
And stores that are frequently
visited by online consumers

00:59:22.545 --> 00:59:24.920 align:middle line:84%
are going to be able to set
higher prices because they're

00:59:24.920 --> 00:59:26.040 align:middle line:90%
more patient.

00:59:26.040 --> 00:59:30.780 align:middle line:84%
Those that have fewer visitors
are going to set lower prices.

00:59:30.780 --> 00:59:33.840 align:middle line:84%
How sensitive prices
are to arrival rates

00:59:33.840 --> 00:59:38.450 align:middle line:84%
depends on the thickness
of the upper tail.

00:59:38.450 --> 00:59:41.540 align:middle line:84%
If you have an extremely
thick tailed distribution,

00:59:41.540 --> 00:59:45.830 align:middle line:84%
like constant times p
to the minus 1, then

00:59:45.830 --> 00:59:47.930 align:middle line:84%
the monopoly price
increases almost

00:59:47.930 --> 00:59:50.030 align:middle line:90%
linearly in arrival rates.

00:59:50.030 --> 00:59:52.220 align:middle line:84%
If you have a very thin
tailed distribution

00:59:52.220 --> 00:59:55.970 align:middle line:84%
with a greater than 1, you know,
things increase more slowly

00:59:55.970 --> 00:59:58.850 align:middle line:90%
with arrival rates.

00:59:58.850 --> 01:00:03.290 align:middle line:84%
And then with if demand has
this constant elasticity form,

01:00:03.290 --> 01:00:08.060 align:middle line:84%
like this, then you get
this very unusual theorem.

01:00:08.060 --> 01:00:10.820 align:middle line:84%
So the theorem is, suppose
the distribution of consumer

01:00:10.820 --> 01:00:12.680 align:middle line:84%
valuations is such
that demand has

01:00:12.680 --> 01:00:15.320 align:middle line:84%
a truncated, constant
elasticity form.

01:00:15.320 --> 01:00:17.990 align:middle line:84%
Suppose the monopoly
price is not

01:00:17.990 --> 01:00:19.850 align:middle line:84%
at the kink of this
demand curve at 1,

01:00:19.850 --> 01:00:22.970 align:middle line:84%
but it's on the
downward sloping part.

01:00:22.970 --> 01:00:27.530 align:middle line:84%
Then the monopoly price
maximizes social welfare,

01:00:27.530 --> 01:00:32.190 align:middle line:84%
and social welfare is the price
numerically, just numerically.

01:00:32.190 --> 01:00:35.420 align:middle line:84%
Like, I want to say, what's
the expected producer

01:00:35.420 --> 01:00:37.950 align:middle line:84%
surplus plus consumer surplus
from selling this book?

01:00:37.950 --> 01:00:40.520 align:middle line:84%
The answer is, if that
book is being sold for $12,

01:00:40.520 --> 01:00:42.620 align:middle line:84%
the answer expected
welfare is $12.

01:00:42.620 --> 01:00:45.080 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, like,
it's the most powerful

01:00:45.080 --> 01:00:47.840 align:middle line:84%
structural estimation
theorem of all time,

01:00:47.840 --> 01:00:50.720 align:middle line:84%
saying, if you want to
estimate the social welfare

01:00:50.720 --> 01:00:53.250 align:middle line:84%
from dynamic sales, you
just write down the price.

01:00:53.250 --> 01:00:55.458 align:middle line:84%
You don't need to compute
the demand elasticities.

01:00:55.458 --> 01:00:57.750 align:middle line:84%
You don't need to figure out
consumer surplus, nothing.

01:00:57.750 --> 01:01:01.040 align:middle line:84%
You just write down the price
and that is the welfare.

01:01:01.040 --> 01:01:03.050 align:middle line:84%
And then there's also
this optimality theorem

01:01:03.050 --> 01:01:06.140 align:middle line:84%
that there's no
monopoly distortion.

01:01:06.140 --> 01:01:10.200 align:middle line:84%
And so, like, why
does this work out?

01:01:10.200 --> 01:01:15.590 align:middle line:84%
You know, obviously
the monopoly price

01:01:15.590 --> 01:01:19.720 align:middle line:90%
maximizes monopolist profits.

01:01:19.720 --> 01:01:22.690 align:middle line:90%
But what is consumer surplus?

01:01:22.690 --> 01:01:27.670 align:middle line:84%
So if I have this distribution
of consumer valuations.

01:01:27.670 --> 01:01:32.860 align:middle line:84%
So I have this distribution of
consumer valuations, F of V,

01:01:32.860 --> 01:01:35.380 align:middle line:84%
and imagine it looks
something like that.

01:01:35.380 --> 01:01:37.360 align:middle line:84%
And suppose I set
some price, pm.

01:01:37.360 --> 01:01:40.110 align:middle line:90%


01:01:40.110 --> 01:01:41.400 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:01:41.400 --> 01:01:47.490 align:middle line:84%
When I sell the good, there's
some consumer surplus.

01:01:47.490 --> 01:01:49.440 align:middle line:90%
What's the consumer surplus?

01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:54.143 align:middle line:84%
That's the expectation of
V minus pm conditional on V

01:01:54.143 --> 01:01:54.810 align:middle line:90%
greater than pm.

01:01:54.810 --> 01:01:58.150 align:middle line:90%


01:01:58.150 --> 01:02:01.840 align:middle line:84%
How does that vary with
the monopoly price?

01:02:01.840 --> 01:02:11.110 align:middle line:84%
Like, in a linear demand curve,
as I set the price really high,

01:02:11.110 --> 01:02:13.600 align:middle line:84%
you know, the expected value
conditional on being bigger

01:02:13.600 --> 01:02:15.910 align:middle line:90%
than this is almost nothing.

01:02:15.910 --> 01:02:19.000 align:middle line:84%
And so consumer surplus
drops substantially

01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:20.580 align:middle line:90%
with the monopoly price.

01:02:20.580 --> 01:02:23.380 align:middle line:84%
But in a constant
elasticity model,

01:02:23.380 --> 01:02:28.120 align:middle line:84%
the expected value of V
minus pm conditional on V

01:02:28.120 --> 01:02:32.560 align:middle line:84%
being bigger than pm
is proportional to pm.

01:02:32.560 --> 01:02:35.680 align:middle line:84%
So in a constant
elasticity demand curve,

01:02:35.680 --> 01:02:40.780 align:middle line:84%
consumer surplus is
proportional to the price.

01:02:40.780 --> 01:02:43.750 align:middle line:84%
So if you set a really,
really high price,

01:02:43.750 --> 01:02:46.010 align:middle line:84%
you don't sell for
a very long time,

01:02:46.010 --> 01:02:49.250 align:middle line:84%
and so the pe to the minus
rt part gets really small.

01:02:49.250 --> 01:02:51.790 align:middle line:84%
But when you do sell it,
the person because they're

01:02:51.790 --> 01:02:53.950 align:middle line:84%
willing to pay $1
million for this book,

01:02:53.950 --> 01:02:58.640 align:middle line:84%
their expected surplus is also
on the order of $1 million.

01:02:58.640 --> 01:03:01.910 align:middle line:84%
And so with constant
elasticity demand,

01:03:01.910 --> 01:03:04.130 align:middle line:84%
the consumer surplus
is just always

01:03:04.130 --> 01:03:07.700 align:middle line:84%
proportional to the price being
paid, expected proportional

01:03:07.700 --> 01:03:08.550 align:middle line:90%
to price.

01:03:08.550 --> 01:03:12.055 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, by maximizing
expected price paid

01:03:12.055 --> 01:03:13.430 align:middle line:84%
discounted expected
price, you're

01:03:13.430 --> 01:03:16.850 align:middle line:84%
also maximizing discounted
expected social welfare.

01:03:16.850 --> 01:03:18.710 align:middle line:90%
So I view this as more a--

01:03:18.710 --> 01:03:21.440 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's a neat
estimation trick or something,

01:03:21.440 --> 01:03:23.540 align:middle line:84%
but I view it more
as a cautionary tale.

01:03:23.540 --> 01:03:26.870 align:middle line:84%
The cautionary tale being,
if in this demand estimation,

01:03:26.870 --> 01:03:31.400 align:middle line:84%
you can't tell the
demand apart from--

01:03:31.400 --> 01:03:34.670 align:middle line:84%
if you can't tell the
valuation curve apart

01:03:34.670 --> 01:03:37.100 align:middle line:84%
from a constant
elasticity demand curve,

01:03:37.100 --> 01:03:39.650 align:middle line:84%
then you're going
to end up finding

01:03:39.650 --> 01:03:41.278 align:middle line:84%
that the expected
welfare is the price,

01:03:41.278 --> 01:03:43.070 align:middle line:84%
regardless of what the
demand elasticity is

01:03:43.070 --> 01:03:45.610 align:middle line:90%
and everything else are.

01:03:45.610 --> 01:03:48.437 align:middle line:84%
So it can be a
first approximation.

01:03:48.437 --> 01:03:50.520 align:middle line:84%
And it's also, if you want
a better approximation,

01:03:50.520 --> 01:03:53.250 align:middle line:84%
you need to be able to tell
the shape of this curve

01:03:53.250 --> 01:03:56.085 align:middle line:84%
apart from a constant
elasticity one, which

01:03:56.085 --> 01:03:57.960 align:middle line:84%
is going to be hard to
do when the upper tail

01:03:57.960 --> 01:04:01.610 align:middle line:84%
because you don't see things
in the upper tail very often.

01:04:01.610 --> 01:04:04.010 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:04.010 --> 01:04:07.340 align:middle line:84%
so now let me think about
that's a monopoly model.

01:04:07.340 --> 01:04:09.600 align:middle line:84%
Again, think about
an oligopoly model.

01:04:09.600 --> 01:04:11.880 align:middle line:84%
So you have two
types of consumers.

01:04:11.880 --> 01:04:14.670 align:middle line:84%
You have some people
who just go straight.

01:04:14.670 --> 01:04:17.360 align:middle line:84%
Like, they know some
online bookstore,

01:04:17.360 --> 01:04:20.420 align:middle line:84%
like Better World Books
or Powell's Books.

01:04:20.420 --> 01:04:23.810 align:middle line:84%
And they just go directly
to PowellsBooks.com,

01:04:23.810 --> 01:04:25.120 align:middle line:90%
look for a book there.

01:04:25.120 --> 01:04:27.800 align:middle line:90%


01:04:27.800 --> 01:04:30.830 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have some
consumers who have this,

01:04:30.830 --> 01:04:32.700 align:middle line:90%
or you do a Google search.

01:04:32.700 --> 01:04:35.480 align:middle line:84%
Google search reveals
the name of one bookstore

01:04:35.480 --> 01:04:36.518 align:middle line:90%
for the title you want.

01:04:36.518 --> 01:04:38.060 align:middle line:84%
You just go to that
bookstore and you

01:04:38.060 --> 01:04:41.050 align:middle line:84%
buy from the first, the one
that's highest ranked on Google.

01:04:41.050 --> 01:04:42.830 align:middle line:84%
And then we have
other people who

01:04:42.830 --> 01:04:45.920 align:middle line:84%
are going to be shoppers who
are going to arrive at a Poisson

01:04:45.920 --> 01:04:49.910 align:middle line:84%
rate and they observe the
prices at all the stores.

01:04:49.910 --> 01:04:52.562 align:middle line:90%
Do I have a picture of AbeBooks?

01:04:52.562 --> 01:04:54.020 align:middle line:84%
I don't have a
picture of AbeBooks.

01:04:54.020 --> 01:04:54.520 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:04:54.520 --> 01:05:01.370 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, we use data in
the paper from a website

01:05:01.370 --> 01:05:02.790 align:middle line:90%
called AbeBooks.com.

01:05:02.790 --> 01:05:08.450 align:middle line:90%


01:05:08.450 --> 01:05:12.810 align:middle line:84%
AbeBooks, it's a used
book aggregator site.

01:05:12.810 --> 01:05:14.040 align:middle line:90%
You can go on there.

01:05:14.040 --> 01:05:17.400 align:middle line:84%
You could type in any book
you want that's out of print.

01:05:17.400 --> 01:05:20.240 align:middle line:84%
It will find 100
online bookstores that

01:05:20.240 --> 01:05:22.660 align:middle line:90%
sell that particular used book.

01:05:22.660 --> 01:05:24.950 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:05:24.950 --> 01:05:26.612 align:middle line:84%
Actually, you
know, AbeBooks used

01:05:26.612 --> 01:05:27.820 align:middle line:90%
to be an independent company.

01:05:27.820 --> 01:05:29.080 align:middle line:90%
It was purchased by Amazon.

01:05:29.080 --> 01:05:31.890 align:middle line:84%
So if you go to the
Amazon Buy Used button,

01:05:31.890 --> 01:05:36.200 align:middle line:84%
Amazon Buy Used button
is the AbeBooks listings.

01:05:36.200 --> 01:05:38.575 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, Amazon invented
the Buy Used button first.

01:05:38.575 --> 01:05:40.200 align:middle line:84%
When they invented
the Buy Used button,

01:05:40.200 --> 01:05:41.940 align:middle line:84%
there was nothing
there under Buy Used.

01:05:41.940 --> 01:05:44.590 align:middle line:84%
You'd click Buy Used and there
were no copies available.

01:05:44.590 --> 01:05:46.340 align:middle line:84%
And then they realized,
well, why don't we

01:05:46.340 --> 01:05:49.140 align:middle line:84%
just buy AbeBooks that has
10 million used books listed

01:05:49.140 --> 01:05:51.430 align:middle line:84%
and put all their listings
under the Buy Used button?

01:05:51.430 --> 01:05:53.097 align:middle line:84%
So now if you go to
the Buy Used button,

01:05:53.097 --> 01:05:55.530 align:middle line:84%
you're seeing the same
AbeBooks listings.

01:05:55.530 --> 01:05:56.640 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:05:56.640 --> 01:05:58.087 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so our
model is that there

01:05:58.087 --> 01:05:59.795 align:middle line:84%
are sort of these two
types of customers,

01:05:59.795 --> 01:06:02.280 align:middle line:84%
the ones going to Amazon
and clicking Buy Used

01:06:02.280 --> 01:06:05.560 align:middle line:84%
and getting this curated list of
all the things sorted by price,

01:06:05.560 --> 01:06:07.560 align:middle line:84%
and then the people who
are searching for a book

01:06:07.560 --> 01:06:10.200 align:middle line:84%
by just typing it into
Google or going directly

01:06:10.200 --> 01:06:12.030 align:middle line:84%
to a used bookstore
that they know online

01:06:12.030 --> 01:06:14.440 align:middle line:90%
and finding it that way.

01:06:14.440 --> 01:06:16.860 align:middle line:84%
You know, we know
that this model

01:06:16.860 --> 01:06:19.953 align:middle line:84%
can have pure strategy dispersed
price equilibrium, not so

01:06:19.953 --> 01:06:21.870 align:middle line:84%
different from the
dispersed price equilibrium

01:06:21.870 --> 01:06:25.670 align:middle line:84%
I did when I did differentiated
product search models.

01:06:25.670 --> 01:06:27.450 align:middle line:84%
You know, the firms
here, the dispersion

01:06:27.450 --> 01:06:30.000 align:middle line:84%
is going to be the firms that
have the higher arrival rates

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:33.440 align:middle line:84%
are going to set high prices and
mostly serve the non-shoppers.

01:06:33.440 --> 01:06:35.340 align:middle line:84%
If you don't have a
lot of non-shoppers,

01:06:35.340 --> 01:06:38.670 align:middle line:84%
you're going to set a low price
and compete for the shoppers.

01:06:38.670 --> 01:06:42.450 align:middle line:84%
And when I'm thinking about,
like, how does the online prices

01:06:42.450 --> 01:06:43.890 align:middle line:90%
differ from the offline prices?

01:06:43.890 --> 01:06:46.030 align:middle line:84%
It helps think that
there are two reasons.

01:06:46.030 --> 01:06:47.762 align:middle line:90%
One is the match quality reason.

01:06:47.762 --> 01:06:50.220 align:middle line:84%
The higher arrival rates shift
the distribution of monopoly

01:06:50.220 --> 01:06:52.710 align:middle line:90%
prices to the right.

01:06:52.710 --> 01:06:54.930 align:middle line:84%
The second is the
competition effect,

01:06:54.930 --> 01:06:57.970 align:middle line:84%
which is there's a desire
to attract the shoppers.

01:06:57.970 --> 01:07:01.020 align:middle line:84%
So the firms that have low
arrival rates of non-shoppers

01:07:01.020 --> 01:07:03.150 align:middle line:84%
are going to be competing
against each other

01:07:03.150 --> 01:07:05.592 align:middle line:84%
to try to sell in a more
Bertrand-like environment

01:07:05.592 --> 01:07:07.550 align:middle line:84%
to the people who are
using the search engines.

01:07:07.550 --> 01:07:10.610 align:middle line:90%


01:07:10.610 --> 01:07:13.200 align:middle line:84%
So I think on the next page
I have this illustration.

01:07:13.200 --> 01:07:16.590 align:middle line:84%
So here's a numerical
simulation of the model.

01:07:16.590 --> 01:07:20.960 align:middle line:84%
So this dashed gray line would
be the distribution of monopoly

01:07:20.960 --> 01:07:22.850 align:middle line:84%
prices if there
are exponentially

01:07:22.850 --> 01:07:24.240 align:middle line:90%
distributed arrival rates.

01:07:24.240 --> 01:07:26.390 align:middle line:84%
And so the firms with
high arrival rates

01:07:26.390 --> 01:07:30.320 align:middle line:84%
at high prices, those with low
arrival rates at low prices.

01:07:30.320 --> 01:07:34.700 align:middle line:84%
If I increase the
value of the good

01:07:34.700 --> 01:07:36.830 align:middle line:84%
because people are getting
a better matched good,

01:07:36.830 --> 01:07:38.390 align:middle line:84%
the monopoly price
distribution would

01:07:38.390 --> 01:07:42.250 align:middle line:84%
shift to the right from the
gray line to the blue line.

01:07:42.250 --> 01:07:45.735 align:middle line:84%
But then if I add in
this atom of shoppers

01:07:45.735 --> 01:07:47.860 align:middle line:84%
who are just going to buy
for the lowest price book

01:07:47.860 --> 01:07:51.040 align:middle line:84%
and are fairly price
insensitive, what you get

01:07:51.040 --> 01:07:52.930 align:middle line:90%
is the distribution of prices.

01:07:52.930 --> 01:07:55.540 align:middle line:84%
All the firms that are going
to be setting the lowest prices

01:07:55.540 --> 01:07:58.180 align:middle line:84%
are now Bertrand competing
against the other firms

01:07:58.180 --> 01:08:00.220 align:middle line:84%
who are also going to
set very low prices.

01:08:00.220 --> 01:08:03.620 align:middle line:84%
And so the low end of the price
distribution gets pulled down.

01:08:03.620 --> 01:08:06.570 align:middle line:84%
The upper tail really
doesn't get affected at all.

01:08:06.570 --> 01:08:08.320 align:middle line:84%
The upper tail doesn't
get affected at all

01:08:08.320 --> 01:08:10.630 align:middle line:84%
because these firms were
just monopoly pricing

01:08:10.630 --> 01:08:13.870 align:middle line:84%
and serving the high
value customers who happen

01:08:13.870 --> 01:08:15.520 align:middle line:90%
on their website occasionally.

01:08:15.520 --> 01:08:17.821 align:middle line:84%
They don't bother to
compete for the shoppers.

01:08:17.821 --> 01:08:19.779 align:middle line:84%
They just leave the high
price and just exploit

01:08:19.779 --> 01:08:21.340 align:middle line:90%
the non-shoppers.

01:08:21.340 --> 01:08:23.710 align:middle line:84%
And so what you get
is this increase

01:08:23.710 --> 01:08:28.060 align:middle line:84%
in dispersion, which comes
from the upper tail really

01:08:28.060 --> 01:08:30.700 align:middle line:84%
having moved up because
people have higher values,

01:08:30.700 --> 01:08:35.600 align:middle line:84%
and then the lower tail getting
pulled down by the competition.

01:08:35.600 --> 01:08:38.170 align:middle line:84%
And so we think those are the
two things you should expect

01:08:38.170 --> 01:08:40.630 align:middle line:84%
is that the online price
distribution has this thicker

01:08:40.630 --> 01:08:43.850 align:middle line:84%
upper tail relative to the
offline price distribution,

01:08:43.850 --> 01:08:47.050 align:middle line:84%
but then it also has more mass
in the lower tail, perhaps.

01:08:47.050 --> 01:08:52.870 align:middle line:90%


01:08:52.870 --> 01:08:55.600 align:middle line:84%
OK, so how do we try to
test this in the data?

01:08:55.600 --> 01:09:00.250 align:middle line:84%
So we gathered a data
set that had 335 books

01:09:00.250 --> 01:09:03.580 align:middle line:84%
representative of traditional
used bookstores' inventories.

01:09:03.580 --> 01:09:07.000 align:middle line:90%
The way this worked is we just--

01:09:07.000 --> 01:09:08.819 align:middle line:84%
actually, Sara did
some of this herself.

01:09:08.819 --> 01:09:12.760 align:middle line:84%
We also just sent an RA to
some physical bookstores with

01:09:12.760 --> 01:09:17.979 align:middle line:84%
the instructions, I want you to
go to the physical bookstores

01:09:17.979 --> 01:09:22.060 align:middle line:84%
and just go to the first
shelf, take every 17th book off

01:09:22.060 --> 01:09:25.210 align:middle line:84%
the shelf, take that book, write
down the title, what it is,

01:09:25.210 --> 01:09:28.479 align:middle line:84%
write down the price, and then
just go shelf to shelf to shelf

01:09:28.479 --> 01:09:31.300 align:middle line:90%
picking out books.

01:09:31.300 --> 01:09:33.460 align:middle line:84%
So we have hundreds,
what we call

01:09:33.460 --> 01:09:35.390 align:middle line:90%
standard books in the data set.

01:09:35.390 --> 01:09:38.960 align:middle line:84%
We also oversampled what we
call local interest books.

01:09:38.960 --> 01:09:40.630 align:middle line:84%
Like, if you go into
a Boston bookstore,

01:09:40.630 --> 01:09:42.880 align:middle line:84%
you'll notice there's
a shelf of books

01:09:42.880 --> 01:09:46.270 align:middle line:90%
about Boston and Boston stuff.

01:09:46.270 --> 01:09:50.319 align:middle line:84%
And so what we said is, if
there's a local interest

01:09:50.319 --> 01:09:53.359 align:middle line:84%
section, so there's a Boston
shelf in a Boston bookstore,

01:09:53.359 --> 01:09:55.790 align:middle line:84%
one of the bookstores
was in Atlanta, there

01:09:55.790 --> 01:09:58.185 align:middle line:84%
if there's a book about
Christmas in Georgia,

01:09:58.185 --> 01:10:00.560 align:middle line:84%
you know, like, find the shelf
that has the Georgia books

01:10:00.560 --> 01:10:03.440 align:middle line:90%
and oversample those.

01:10:03.440 --> 01:10:06.580 align:middle line:84%
And then we also separate
out popular books.

01:10:06.580 --> 01:10:08.450 align:middle line:84%
Popular books are
the things like--

01:10:08.450 --> 01:10:10.040 align:middle line:84%
I mean, not necessarily
Harry Potter,

01:10:10.040 --> 01:10:11.720 align:middle line:84%
but things that
are popular enough

01:10:11.720 --> 01:10:14.728 align:middle line:84%
that every used bookstore is
probably going to have a copy.

01:10:14.728 --> 01:10:17.270 align:middle line:84%
There are some things that these
are former mega bestsellers.

01:10:17.270 --> 01:10:19.490 align:middle line:84%
So it's not so
rare to find these,

01:10:19.490 --> 01:10:22.130 align:middle line:84%
whereas the standard books
are all going to be completely

01:10:22.130 --> 01:10:24.680 align:middle line:84%
non-overlapping across stores
because just the average used

01:10:24.680 --> 01:10:26.013 align:middle line:90%
book--

01:10:26.013 --> 01:10:27.180 align:middle line:90%
there are millions of books.

01:10:27.180 --> 01:10:28.850 align:middle line:84%
The average used book
is just no one's going

01:10:28.850 --> 01:10:30.090 align:middle line:90%
to have another copy of that.

01:10:30.090 --> 01:10:31.965 align:middle line:84%
You could search the
bookstores all your life

01:10:31.965 --> 01:10:34.720 align:middle line:84%
and probably not find another
copy of a standard book.

01:10:34.720 --> 01:10:35.600 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:10:35.600 --> 01:10:38.700 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so we have
four data collections.

01:10:38.700 --> 01:10:41.420 align:middle line:84%
What we did is we first just
randomly selected these books

01:10:41.420 --> 01:10:43.310 align:middle line:84%
from physical
bookstores, and then we

01:10:43.310 --> 01:10:47.450 align:middle line:84%
went online and searched for
those books at AbeBooks.com.

01:10:47.450 --> 01:10:52.160 align:middle line:84%
And then we noticed
that in between the time

01:10:52.160 --> 01:10:54.470 align:middle line:84%
we collected this and
we wrote the paper,

01:10:54.470 --> 01:10:57.050 align:middle line:90%
Amazon bought AbeBooks.

01:10:57.050 --> 01:11:00.320 align:middle line:84%
And we thought, wow, that's
a great second accidental

01:11:00.320 --> 01:11:03.800 align:middle line:84%
opportunity here is that Amazon
buying AbeBooks and putting

01:11:03.800 --> 01:11:06.000 align:middle line:84%
the listings under
Buy Used probably

01:11:06.000 --> 01:11:08.000 align:middle line:84%
just dramatically increased
the number of people

01:11:08.000 --> 01:11:12.960 align:middle line:84%
who are looking at the curated
search engine listings for them.

01:11:12.960 --> 01:11:16.550 align:middle line:84%
And so then after Amazon bought
and incorporated the AbeBooks

01:11:16.550 --> 01:11:19.140 align:middle line:84%
listings, we went out and
collected the data again

01:11:19.140 --> 01:11:20.720 align:middle line:90%
a second time.

01:11:20.720 --> 01:11:22.780 align:middle line:84%
And we collected the data
again a second time so

01:11:22.780 --> 01:11:25.700 align:middle line:84%
that we would have a before
and after on prices, before

01:11:25.700 --> 01:11:27.980 align:middle line:90%
and after the AbeBooks listings.

01:11:27.980 --> 01:11:30.270 align:middle line:84%
And then we went and
collected the data fourth time

01:11:30.270 --> 01:11:31.520 align:middle line:90%
a few months later.

01:11:31.520 --> 01:11:34.340 align:middle line:84%
We realized it would be
useful to have quantity data,

01:11:34.340 --> 01:11:36.470 align:middle line:84%
and a way to get
quantity data is

01:11:36.470 --> 01:11:38.690 align:middle line:84%
to search a bookstore
for the book,

01:11:38.690 --> 01:11:40.838 align:middle line:84%
search that same bookstore
two months later,

01:11:40.838 --> 01:11:42.380 align:middle line:84%
see if they're still
listing the book

01:11:42.380 --> 01:11:44.270 align:middle line:84%
and infer if they're
not still listing

01:11:44.270 --> 01:11:47.362 align:middle line:84%
the book two months later,
they may have sold the book.

01:11:47.362 --> 01:11:49.320 align:middle line:84%
Now they may have just
also just taken it down,

01:11:49.320 --> 01:11:52.070 align:middle line:84%
but it's a pain to take
things down from your website.

01:11:52.070 --> 01:11:55.320 align:middle line:84%
You probably take them
down when you sell them.

01:11:55.320 --> 01:11:58.700 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, that's so we have
these four data collections.

01:11:58.700 --> 01:12:01.450 align:middle line:84%
And last two is how
we got quantity data.

01:12:01.450 --> 01:12:04.610 align:middle line:90%


01:12:04.610 --> 01:12:07.230 align:middle line:84%
So what are the predictions
we're trying to test?

01:12:07.230 --> 01:12:10.610 align:middle line:84%
Prediction 1 is
that online prices

01:12:10.610 --> 01:12:12.770 align:middle line:84%
should be more dispersed
than offline prices

01:12:12.770 --> 01:12:16.000 align:middle line:90%
and have a thicker upper tail.

01:12:16.000 --> 01:12:19.870 align:middle line:84%
Prediction 2 is, local
interest books may already

01:12:19.870 --> 01:12:22.360 align:middle line:84%
be well matched by
the offline stores,

01:12:22.360 --> 01:12:25.330 align:middle line:84%
and so the online-offline
gap should be smaller

01:12:25.330 --> 01:12:27.110 align:middle line:90%
for the local interest books.

01:12:27.110 --> 01:12:30.163 align:middle line:84%
In particular, I put
this quote up here.

01:12:30.163 --> 01:12:32.080 align:middle line:84%
I found this online
review of one of the books

01:12:32.080 --> 01:12:34.240 align:middle line:84%
in our data set, the Mount
Vernon Street Warrens--

01:12:34.240 --> 01:12:35.030 align:middle line:90%
A Boston Story.

01:12:35.030 --> 01:12:37.360 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you guys
have been to Beacon Hill.

01:12:37.360 --> 01:12:41.650 align:middle line:84%
Mount Vernon is one of the
streets on Beacon Hill.

01:12:41.650 --> 01:12:43.690 align:middle line:84%
It's a cute street or
whatever, with the gas

01:12:43.690 --> 01:12:46.420 align:middle line:84%
lamps and the brick
sidewalks and stuff.

01:12:46.420 --> 01:12:50.170 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, if you go into a local
Boston bookstore, one of them,

01:12:50.170 --> 01:12:52.960 align:middle line:84%
some RA found a copy of this
Mount Vernon Street Warrens.

01:12:52.960 --> 01:12:54.700 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, if you read
that book, like,

01:12:54.700 --> 01:12:56.020 align:middle line:90%
what's your overwhelming thing?

01:12:56.020 --> 01:12:57.970 align:middle line:84%
So this is, you know,
"telling the stories

01:12:57.970 --> 01:13:00.178 align:middle line:84%
of these children who became
notable for eccentricity

01:13:00.178 --> 01:13:00.890 align:middle line:90%
and philanthropy.

01:13:00.890 --> 01:13:03.940 align:middle line:84%
Green focuses on Ned Warren,
a homosexual and mover

01:13:03.940 --> 01:13:06.010 align:middle line:84%
in the international
movement of aestheticism,

01:13:06.010 --> 01:13:08.677 align:middle line:84%
who was determined to lead
a 'grand but blighted life.'

01:13:08.677 --> 01:13:11.260 align:middle line:84%
This somewhat jumbled tale of a
family sundering through greed

01:13:11.260 --> 01:13:12.350 align:middle line:90%
and suicide."

01:13:12.350 --> 01:13:14.650 align:middle line:84%
You know, basically I
read this and I'm like,

01:13:14.650 --> 01:13:16.140 align:middle line:90%
I don't want to read that book.

01:13:16.140 --> 01:13:18.560 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I don't know so much
about what aestheticism was,

01:13:18.560 --> 01:13:21.670 align:middle line:84%
but how much do I want to really
read about greed and suicide

01:13:21.670 --> 01:13:22.920 align:middle line:90%
in Boston in the 19th century?

01:13:22.920 --> 01:13:25.860 align:middle line:84%
And the whole point is, in fact,
nobody wants to read that book.

01:13:25.860 --> 01:13:28.260 align:middle line:84%
So who is the high value
purchaser of that book?

01:13:28.260 --> 01:13:31.040 align:middle line:84%
A tourist who's just been to
Beacon Hill and seen Mount

01:13:31.040 --> 01:13:35.360 align:middle line:84%
Vernon Street and finds a
copy of a book about a family

01:13:35.360 --> 01:13:37.820 align:middle line:84%
on Mount Vernon Street in the
19th century and thinks, wow,

01:13:37.820 --> 01:13:39.660 align:middle line:84%
this will be great to
read in my hotel room.

01:13:39.660 --> 01:13:41.480 align:middle line:84%
And then you get partway through
and get the suicide side,

01:13:41.480 --> 01:13:43.522 align:middle line:84%
and you're like, this is
not my vacation reading.

01:13:43.522 --> 01:13:45.290 align:middle line:84%
And then you leave
it there and then

01:13:45.290 --> 01:13:47.300 align:middle line:84%
the hotel turns it back
in and then sells it

01:13:47.300 --> 01:13:49.133 align:middle line:84%
to the used bookstore,
and then they sell it

01:13:49.133 --> 01:13:51.380 align:middle line:84%
again to the next
tourist or something.

01:13:51.380 --> 01:13:53.270 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, but so
for these books,

01:13:53.270 --> 01:13:55.580 align:middle line:84%
like, the online price
is not really much higher

01:13:55.580 --> 01:13:59.120 align:middle line:84%
because no one is out there
searching for that book.

01:13:59.120 --> 01:14:03.980 align:middle line:84%
But it is a good match to
the local physical bookstore.

01:14:03.980 --> 01:14:06.980 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, other predictions,
the online-offline gap

01:14:06.980 --> 01:14:09.080 align:middle line:84%
should also be smaller
for popular titles

01:14:09.080 --> 01:14:11.960 align:middle line:84%
because there's a thinner
upper tail for popular titles.

01:14:11.960 --> 01:14:14.100 align:middle line:84%
There's not the
match quality issue.

01:14:14.100 --> 01:14:17.240 align:middle line:84%
And then Amazon's
Buy Used feature

01:14:17.240 --> 01:14:19.790 align:middle line:84%
should lead to more
low prices in 2012

01:14:19.790 --> 01:14:23.330 align:middle line:84%
because it increases that
competition for the lower tail.

01:14:23.330 --> 01:14:26.010 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so those are the
predictions we try to test.

01:14:26.010 --> 01:14:28.670 align:middle line:84%
And let me just say
basically the story is,

01:14:28.670 --> 01:14:31.100 align:middle line:84%
almost all of these things
seem to be true in the data.

01:14:31.100 --> 01:14:33.470 align:middle line:84%
In each of these
figures, the left

01:14:33.470 --> 01:14:37.100 align:middle line:84%
is the distribution
of offline prices.

01:14:37.100 --> 01:14:41.060 align:middle line:84%
The right side is an estimate
of the same object, an estimate

01:14:41.060 --> 01:14:44.480 align:middle line:84%
of the average across titles
of the distribution of prices

01:14:44.480 --> 01:14:46.160 align:middle line:90%
for that title.

01:14:46.160 --> 01:14:50.120 align:middle line:84%
So these should be estimates
of the distribution

01:14:50.120 --> 01:14:52.100 align:middle line:84%
of offline prices
for these books

01:14:52.100 --> 01:14:54.480 align:middle line:84%
and the distribution of
online prices for these books.

01:14:54.480 --> 01:14:56.090 align:middle line:84%
And if you see for
the standard books,

01:14:56.090 --> 01:14:59.930 align:middle line:84%
there's just this massively
higher upper tail of prices

01:14:59.930 --> 01:15:04.600 align:middle line:90%
online than there was offline.

01:15:04.600 --> 01:15:06.730 align:middle line:84%
If you go to the
local interest books,

01:15:06.730 --> 01:15:11.140 align:middle line:84%
the local interest books
already had something of a tail

01:15:11.140 --> 01:15:13.220 align:middle line:84%
online because the ones
that are well-matched.

01:15:13.220 --> 01:15:15.670 align:middle line:84%
That book that finds its way
to the Beacon Hill bookstore

01:15:15.670 --> 01:15:18.220 align:middle line:84%
is already well-matched,
can charge a very high price

01:15:18.220 --> 01:15:19.310 align:middle line:90%
offline.

01:15:19.310 --> 01:15:21.830 align:middle line:90%
And so the offline--

01:15:21.830 --> 01:15:24.410 align:middle line:84%
you know, more of an
upper tail offline.

01:15:24.410 --> 01:15:27.170 align:middle line:84%
The online-offline gap is
smaller for those local interest

01:15:27.170 --> 01:15:28.820 align:middle line:90%
books.

01:15:28.820 --> 01:15:33.200 align:middle line:84%
Popular ones also
works, but we skip that.

01:15:33.200 --> 01:15:36.690 align:middle line:90%
This is a 2009-2012 comparison.

01:15:36.690 --> 01:15:42.800 align:middle line:84%
2009-2012 comparison, the
gray bars are the 2009 price

01:15:42.800 --> 01:15:43.650 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

01:15:43.650 --> 01:15:47.880 align:middle line:84%
The white outlined bars
for the 2012 distribution.

01:15:47.880 --> 01:15:51.020 align:middle line:84%
And you see these
distributions almost

01:15:51.020 --> 01:15:53.210 align:middle line:84%
lie exactly on
top of each other,

01:15:53.210 --> 01:15:56.360 align:middle line:84%
with the exception that
here on the standard titles,

01:15:56.360 --> 01:15:59.840 align:middle line:84%
the number of standard titles
sold at a very low price just

01:15:59.840 --> 01:16:04.520 align:middle line:84%
shoots up in 2012 after
the Amazon acquisition.

01:16:04.520 --> 01:16:06.990 align:middle line:84%
And so this is if there are
many more people searching.

01:16:06.990 --> 01:16:09.500 align:middle line:84%
And so that left tail
just gets pushed down,

01:16:09.500 --> 01:16:11.910 align:middle line:84%
but it doesn't affect
the upper tail at all.

01:16:11.910 --> 01:16:14.270 align:middle line:84%
And you see that in all
three categories of books

01:16:14.270 --> 01:16:17.583 align:middle line:84%
that the lower tail
gets much thicker.

01:16:17.583 --> 01:16:20.000 align:middle line:84%
And it just seems to be drawing
from these slightly higher

01:16:20.000 --> 01:16:24.780 align:middle line:84%
prices, as if it's competition
is pulling down the bottom end.

01:16:24.780 --> 01:16:28.200 align:middle line:84%
And then, you know,
the paper also

01:16:28.200 --> 01:16:30.810 align:middle line:84%
then develops a structural
model to estimate

01:16:30.810 --> 01:16:34.320 align:middle line:84%
what are the welfare gains
from selling books online.

01:16:34.320 --> 01:16:37.710 align:middle line:84%
And our answer is, there seems
to be a substantial welfare

01:16:37.710 --> 01:16:41.530 align:middle line:84%
gain, but even in this used
books case, it's not enormous.

01:16:41.530 --> 01:16:43.380 align:middle line:84%
So we're more with
Quan and Williams

01:16:43.380 --> 01:16:45.660 align:middle line:84%
than we are with
Brynjolfsson, Hu, and Smith

01:16:45.660 --> 01:16:47.520 align:middle line:84%
in terms of what are
the welfare gains.

01:16:47.520 --> 01:16:54.960 align:middle line:84%
What we find is that the
estimated social welfare

01:16:54.960 --> 01:16:59.100 align:middle line:84%
from selling one of these 2009
books in the offline world

01:16:59.100 --> 01:17:02.100 align:middle line:90%
was $7.76.

01:17:02.100 --> 01:17:07.720 align:middle line:84%
Estimated social welfare from
selling it online was $16.87.

01:17:07.720 --> 01:17:10.680 align:middle line:84%
So it's a more than
doubling of welfare

01:17:10.680 --> 01:17:14.970 align:middle line:84%
that comes from selling
online, but it's only

01:17:14.970 --> 01:17:16.390 align:middle line:90%
a more than doubling of welfare.

01:17:16.390 --> 01:17:19.980 align:middle line:84%
It's not a massive, not a
10 times increase in welfare

01:17:19.980 --> 01:17:21.730 align:middle line:84%
from selling these
things online.

01:17:21.730 --> 01:17:24.665 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at
the firm profits,

01:17:24.665 --> 01:17:26.790 align:middle line:84%
the profit is coming from
two different dimensions.

01:17:26.790 --> 01:17:31.370 align:middle line:84%
It's like the average price
is going from $12 to $16

01:17:31.370 --> 01:17:35.700 align:middle line:84%
when they're sold online and
the books are being sold sooner.

01:17:35.700 --> 01:17:38.450 align:middle line:84%
So instead of the e to
the minus rt being 0.3,

01:17:38.450 --> 01:17:41.150 align:middle line:90%
the e to the minus rt is 0.48.

01:17:41.150 --> 01:17:44.750 align:middle line:84%
So the books are getting
sold for higher prices

01:17:44.750 --> 01:17:46.590 align:middle line:90%
and they're getting sold sooner.

01:17:46.590 --> 01:17:50.000 align:middle line:84%
Those two things both make
profit more than double.

01:17:50.000 --> 01:17:53.660 align:middle line:84%
We're also estimating that the
consumer surplus, as best we

01:17:53.660 --> 01:17:56.870 align:middle line:90%
can, is also more than doubling.

01:17:56.870 --> 01:17:58.430 align:middle line:84%
And the consumer
surplus is more than

01:17:58.430 --> 01:18:02.477 align:middle line:84%
doubling because the people who
are willing to buy $16 books

01:18:02.477 --> 01:18:05.060 align:middle line:84%
get more utility than people who
are willing to buy $12 books,

01:18:05.060 --> 01:18:06.330 align:middle line:90%
and they're getting them sooner.

01:18:06.330 --> 01:18:07.872 align:middle line:84%
So the consumer
surplus is discounted

01:18:07.872 --> 01:18:09.740 align:middle line:90%
by this instead of that.

01:18:09.740 --> 01:18:10.240 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:18:10.240 --> 01:18:16.036 align:middle line:90%


01:18:16.036 --> 01:18:17.200 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, I'm out of time.

01:18:17.200 --> 01:18:18.930 align:middle line:84%
So [INAUDIBLE] you
say, that's what

01:18:18.930 --> 01:18:20.190 align:middle line:90%
I've got on retail for today.

01:18:20.190 --> 01:18:21.950 align:middle line:90%
And then--

01:18:21.950 --> 01:18:34.000 align:middle line:90%